= Centrifugation
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

<Centrifugation> separates suspended material by its motion relative to a rotating liquid. A <centrifuge rotor> creates an outward <centrifugal acceleration> in the <rotating reference frame>; material denser than its surroundings tends to move outward, opposed by <drag force>. Collected material can form a <centrifugation pellet>, leaving a <supernatant>, or remain in separated zones within a <density gradient>. The useful question is which physical difference the separation exploits: a difference in <sedimentation coefficient> or a difference in <buoyant density>.

\Image[centrifuge-rotor-and-separated-fractions.png]
{title=<Centrifuge rotor> geometry and the <centrifugation pellet>–<supernatant> distinction}
{description=Original schematic of one swung-out tube during rotation and a conceptual pelleting comparison. <Rotor radius> increases toward the tube's outer end. Colors and particle counts illustrate incomplete separation and trapping; they are not measured recovery fractions. The tube remains horizontal in this depiction of the rotating geometry.}

<Centrifugation> makes otherwise slow settling useful for studying <biological cells>, <organelles>, <proteins> and <DNA>. Separation and identification are different steps: a <centrifugation pellet> is a collected fraction, not proof that it contains only one kind of <organelle>. The <lysozyme wall-removal experiment> illustrates a complementary readout: testing material released into the separated liquid can be more informative than inspecting the retained solids.

= Microcentrifuge
{parent=Centrifugation}

A <microcentrifuge> performs <centrifugation> of small sample tubes, as used to concentrate <biological cells> and separate debris in the <PV92 teaching experiment>.

= Centrifuge rotor
{parent=Centrifugation}

A <centrifuge rotor> holds sample vessels around the rotation axis. A fixed-angle <centrifuge rotor> holds tubes at a fixed inclination; a swinging-bucket <centrifuge rotor> lets them swing outward during rotation. Material moves away from the axis, so a <centrifugation pellet> can first collect against an outer tube wall rather than travel along the entire tube axis. Geometry sets both the <rotor radius> and the travel distance.<Basics: Centrifugal Force & Centrifuge Type by Eppendorf>[Basics: Centrifugal Force & Centrifuge Type, Fixed-angle or swing-bucket rotors? and Fixed-angle rotor: tube orientation and pellet position]{ref}

= Rotor radius
{parent=Centrifuge rotor}
{title2=$r$}

The <rotor radius> $r$ is the perpendicular distance from the rotation axis to a specified sample position, not the tube's length. A filled tube spans a range $r_{\min}\leq r\leq r_{\max}$. Consequently, one rotational speed produces different local <centrifugal accelerations> along that range. A reported <relative centrifugal force> needs a stated reference position, such as the outermost sample radius.

= Relative centrifugal force
{parent=Centrifuge rotor}
{title2=$\mathrm{RCF}=a/g_0$}

<Relative centrifugal force> is the <dimensionless> ratio of the local <centrifugal acceleration> magnitude $a$ to the conventional gravitational reference $g_0=9.80665\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. Despite its name, it is an acceleration ratio: the actual <force> on a specified <mass> $m$ is $ma$. With <angular speed> $\omega$ and <rotor radius> $r$, $a=\omega^2r$. If $n$ is the numerical rotational speed in revolutions per minute and $r_{\mathrm{cm}}$ the numerical <rotor radius> in centimetres, unit conversion gives
$$
\omega=\frac{2\pi n}{60}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}},\qquad
\mathrm{RCF}=\frac{(r_{\mathrm{cm}}/100)(2\pi n/60)^2}{9.80665}
\simeq1.118\times10^{-5}r_{\mathrm{cm}}n^2.
$$
Thus rpm alone does not specify the <centrifugal acceleration>.<Basics: Centrifugal Force & Centrifuge Type by Eppendorf>[Important definitions, Rcm vs rpm, and How to calculate relative centrifugal force: radius dependence and acceleration ratio]{ref}

\Image[centrifuge-radius-speed-relative-acceleration.png]
{title=<Relative centrifugal force> for stipulated <rotor radii> and rotational speeds}
{description=Original theoretical curves calculated from $a=\omega^2r$ with $g_0=9.80665\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. The <rotor radii> of $5$, $10$ and $15\,\mathrm{cm}$ are chosen examples, not specifications of real <centrifuge rotors>. The plotted range is not an instrument operating limit or a recommendation.}

For a stipulated $3000\,\mathrm{rpm}$, <rotor radii> of $5$ and $10\,\mathrm{cm}$ give approximately $503$ and $1006$ times $g_0$. Doubling the <rotor radius> doubles <relative centrifugal force>; doubling rpm quadruples it. Even equal <relative centrifugal forces> do not guarantee equal separation: travel distance, elapsed time, <viscosity>, sample <density> and particle shape still matter. These are comparisons of a <mathematical model>, not instructions for running an instrument.

= Centrifugation pellet
{parent=Centrifugation}

A <centrifugation pellet> is material accumulated against the vessel wall by <centrifugation>. Its existence establishes collection, not purity; other suspended material can be trapped or co-sediment.

= Supernatant
{parent=Centrifugation}

A <supernatant> is the liquid remaining above settled or pelleted material. It can contain dissolved <molecules> and particles that have not reached the <centrifugation pellet>; a visually clear <supernatant> need not be chemically empty.

= Sedimentation coefficient
{parent=Centrifugation}
{title2=$s=v/(\omega^2r)$}

The <sedimentation coefficient> $s$ is radial drift <velocity> divided by local <centrifugal acceleration>. It has units of time and combines particle properties with those of the surrounding liquid. In a dilute, deterministic <mathematical model> with negligible particle <inertia>, an effective displaced volume $V$, particle <mass> $m$, local liquid <density> $\rho_f$ and <linear friction coefficient> $f>0$ give
$$
f v=(m-\rho_f V)\omega^2r,\qquad s=\frac{v}{\omega^2r}=\frac{m-\rho_f V}{f}.
$$
The subtraction represents <buoyancy>. Equal <mass> does not imply equal $s$: changing shape changes <drag force>, and changing the medium changes both <buoyancy> and <viscosity>.https://www.beckman.com/resources/technologies/centrifugation/ultracentrifugation/[An Introduction to Ultracentrifugation, The Physics Behind the Technology: mass, size, shape, medium density and viscosity]{ref}

For an isolated <sphere> of radius $b$ and uniform <density> $\rho_p$, <Stokes's law> supplies $f=6\pi\eta b$, where $\eta$ is dynamic <viscosity>, and $m=(4\pi/3)b^3\rho_p$. Therefore
$$
s=\frac{2b^2(\rho_p-\rho_f)}{9\eta}.
$$
This original force-balance check predicts fourfold $s$ on doubling $b$ with everything else fixed, and zero drift when $\rho_p=\rho_f$. It applies to dilute isolated <spheres> in <Stokes flow>, not arbitrary folded <proteins> or crowded suspensions. If $s$ and $\omega$ remain constant in a uniform medium, the <ordinary differential equation> $dr/dt=s\omega^2r$ has the <exponential function> solution $r(t)=r(0)e^{s\omega^2t}$. Arrival at an outer boundary therefore depends on starting position as well as $s$; a separation has no universal size cutoff.

= Differential centrifugation
{parent=Centrifugation}
{wiki}

<Differential centrifugation> successively separates <centrifugation pellets> from <supernatants> by differences in settling rate. A first <centrifugation pellet> can enrich faster-settling material; a later <centrifugation pellet> from its <supernatant> can enrich slower-settling material. The fractions overlap because particles have different starting positions and can become trapped. <Differential centrifugation> is consequently an enrichment method, not automatic identification or complete purification.https://www.beckman.com/resources/technologies/centrifugation/principles/particle-separation/[Particle Separation: differential pelleting, smaller particles initially near the tube bottom, and incomplete separation]{ref}

= Density-gradient centrifugation
{parent=Centrifugation}

<Density-gradient centrifugation> uses a liquid whose <density> varies along the separation direction. A band does not by itself establish which mechanism produced it: <rate-zonal centrifugation> separates by migration rate before equilibrium, whereas <isopycnic centrifugation> uses equilibrium positions set by <buoyant density>.

The distinction is between a moving clock and a restoring balance. In <rate-zonal centrifugation>, two species may occupy different positions because their <sedimentation coefficients> differ, even when their <buoyant densities> agree. In <isopycnic centrifugation>, two differently sized species can converge to the same band because their <buoyant densities> agree. Conversely, equal <mass> does not ensure equal migration <velocity>: shape affects <drag force>, and medium <density> affects <buoyancy>.<An Introduction to Ultracentrifugation by Beckman Coulter Life Sciences>[The Physics Behind the Technology; Rate Zonal Centrifugation; Isopycnic Centrifugation: migration properties versus matching density]{ref}

The worked <finite-time rate-zonal separation> and <linear-gradient isopycnic focusing> examples isolate these mechanisms in chosen <mathematical models>. The actual <DNA> experiment described under <isopycnic centrifugation> illustrates the second mechanism; its initial collection of <biological cells> into <centrifugation pellets> is a different operation from the later molecular banding. Collecting liquid fractions from a tube can recover material at different positions, but the position alone does not identify its <molecules> or establish purity.

= Isopycnic centrifugation
{parent=Density-gradient centrifugation}

= Equilibrium density-gradient centrifugation
{synonym}

In <isopycnic centrifugation>, a species concentrates near the position where the liquid's <density> matches its <buoyant density>. In a <density gradient> increasing outward, material displaced inward encounters less-dense liquid and tends to move outward; material displaced outward encounters denser liquid and tends to move inward. <Diffusion> broadens the resulting band. If the <density gradient> never reaches the species' <buoyant density>, a matching equilibrium position need not exist within the vessel.https://www.beckman.com/resources/technologies/centrifugation/ultracentrifugation/[An Introduction to Ultracentrifugation, Isopycnic Centrifugation: matching buoyant density and diffusion in equilibrium gradients]{ref}

The 1958 paper <The replication of DNA in Escherichia coli by Matthew Meselson and Franklin W. Stahl> (pp. 672–676, Figures 1–4) makes the distinction concrete. Its separation apparatus was a Spinco Model E ultracentrifuge with an <Escherichia coli> lysate in a <cesium chloride density gradient>. Ultraviolet <wave absorption> photographs of the rotating sample were scanned with a recording microdensitometer: peak position reported <buoyant density>, while signal above baseline reported <DNA> <concentration>.<The replication of DNA in Escherichia coli by Matthew Meselson and Franklin W. Stahl>[p. 676, Experimental: Spinco Model E and recording microdensitometer; p. 675, Figure 4: peak position and baseline]{ref}

Cultures received labeled <nitrogen>; chilled <biological cells> were collected as <centrifugation pellets> and disrupted before molecular banding.<The replication of DNA in Escherichia coli by Matthew Meselson and Franklin W. Stahl>[pp. 673–674, Experimental: labeled growth medium, chilled collection and detergent disruption]{ref}

Mixed lysates labeled fully with the <nitrogen> <isotope> $^{15}\mathrm{N}$ and unlabeled lysates supplied reference bands; a mixture including intermediate material checked its position relative to those references. Figure 1 followed band formation over time, helping distinguish a migrating distribution from an approximately stationary one. These controls address resolution, calibration and approach to equilibrium. They do not make every ultraviolet-absorbing component identifiable as <DNA>, or turn a band into a direct image of individual strands. The <DNA replication> article develops the copying inference; here the key result is a separation by <buoyant density>, not a claim that heavier-labeled <DNA> is physically larger.<The replication of DNA in Escherichia coli by Matthew Meselson and Franklin W. Stahl>[pp. 672–673, Figures 1–2; p. 675, Figure 4 density references and microdensitometer baseline; p. 676, Experimental: Spinco Model E and ultraviolet photographs]{ref}

= Linear-gradient isopycnic focusing
{parent=Isopycnic centrifugation}
{title2=$z_*=(\rho_b-\rho_0)/G$}

Why does an <isopycnic centrifugation> band return after displacement? Choose a dilute <mathematical model> with outward coordinate $z$, constant local <centrifugal acceleration> $a>0$, fixed displaced <volume> $V>0$, <linear friction coefficient> $f>0$, and an imposed liquid <density> $\rho_f(z)=\rho_0+Gz$ with $G>0$. Treat the species' <buoyant density> $\rho_b$ as constant in this medium. The <sedimentation coefficient> force balance gives the <ordinary differential equation>
$$
\frac{dz}{dt}=\frac{Va}{f}\bigl(\rho_b-\rho_f(z)\bigr)=-k(z-z_*),\qquad k=\frac{VaG}{f},\qquad z_*=\frac{\rho_b-\rho_0}{G}.
$$
Its <exponential function> solution is $z(t)=z_*+(z(0)-z_*)e^{-kt}$. The sign of the <velocity> reverses across $z_*$, proving <stable equilibrium> when $z_*$ lies inside the liquid. Changing $V/f$ changes the approach time $1/k$, while $z_*$ depends on <buoyant density> and the imposed <density gradient>. A real <centrifuge rotor> has radially varying <centrifugal acceleration>; the constant-$a$ approximation describes a short radial interval. Here the <density gradient> is held fixed, rather than modeling its formation.

For an original <dimensionless> example, let $u=z/L$ and $\tau=t/T$, where $L$ is column length and $T$ a chosen time scale. Write $q=z_*/L$ and $\lambda=kT$, so $u(\tau)=q+(u(0)-q)e^{-\lambda\tau}$. Species A and B have the same $q=0.45$ but different friction-dependent rates: $(\lambda_A,u_A(0))=(0.4,0.1)$ and $(\lambda_B,u_B(0))=(1.6,0.95)$. A moves outward and B inward, both toward $u=0.45$. Species C has $(q_C,\lambda_C,u_C(0))=(0.8,0.8,0.1)$ and approaches a separate band at $u=0.8$. At $\tau=4$, their positions are approximately $0.3793$, $0.4508$ and $0.7715$: a finite-time photograph would still show A short of its limiting position.

\Image[isopycnic-bands-and-restoring-trajectories.png]
{title=<Isopycnic centrifugation>: different approach rates can lead to the same <buoyant density> band}
{description=Original schematic tubes and deterministic trajectories for the stipulated <linear-gradient isopycnic focusing> model. A and B share a matching <density> position; C has another. Tube shading represents an imposed increasing <density gradient>. Band thickness is schematic; <diffusion> is omitted from the plotted trajectories. The final tube shows the mathematical limit while rotation continues, not a measured result or a stopped tube.}

An equilibrium band has finite width because <diffusion> opposes focusing. In the same local model, let $c(z)$ be <concentration> and let the constant <diffusion coefficient> be $D>0$. Combining the restoring drift with <Fick's laws>, zero net <flux> requires
$$
J=-k(z-z_*)c-D\frac{dc}{dz}=0,\qquad c(z)=c(z_*)\exp\!\left[-\frac{k(z-z_*)^2}{2D}\right].
$$
Dividing by $c$ and integrating proves the <Gaussian function> profile, whose <standard deviation> is $\sigma_z=\sqrt{D/k}$. This local result assumes that the band is narrow relative to its distance from the boundaries and that $D$, $k$ and the <density gradient> vary negligibly across it. Equal <buoyant densities> therefore imply coincident centers in this model, not identical widths. A band outside the available <density> range has no such interior restoring point.

= Buoyant density
{parent=Isopycnic centrifugation}
{title2=$\rho_b$}

<Buoyant density> is the surrounding liquid <density> at which a species has no net centrifugal drift under the stated conditions. It depends on interactions with the medium, including associated solvent, rather than necessarily equaling the dry substance's <density>. Equal <buoyant densities> can give overlapping bands even for differently sized <proteins> or <DNA> fragments.https://www.beckman.com/resources/technologies/centrifugation/ultracentrifugation/[An Introduction to Ultracentrifugation, Isopycnic Centrifugation: buoyant density depends on the surrounding medium]{ref}

= Cesium chloride density gradient
{parent=Isopycnic centrifugation}

A <cesium chloride density gradient> forms when the <salt> $\mathrm{CsCl}$ redistributes in a rotating <chemical solution>. It can supply matching-density positions for <DNA> during <isopycnic centrifugation>.https://www.beckman.com/resources/technologies/centrifugation/density-gradient-centrifugation/[Types of Density Gradients and Types of Gradient Material: self-forming CsCl gradients and nucleic-acid separation]{ref}

= Rate-zonal centrifugation
{parent=Density-gradient centrifugation}

<Rate-zonal centrifugation> resolves an initially narrow sample zone by differences in <sedimentation coefficient> during a finite interval. Its bands remain transient: extending the interval can carry material into a <centrifugation pellet>. Thus a <density gradient> need not imply an equilibrium <buoyant density> measurement.https://www.beckman.com/resources/technologies/centrifugation/density-gradient-centrifugation/[Types of Density Gradients, Rate Zonal Centrifugation and Table 1: migration rates before equilibrium or complete sedimentation]{ref}

A typical preparation layers a narrow sample above a preformed <density gradient>, often containing <sucrose>. The initial zone must be narrow enough that differences in starting position do not overwhelm the later separation. The run ends while useful bands remain inside the liquid. In the simple case where all particles are denser than every part of the column, none has an interior <isopycnic centrifugation> position.<An Introduction to Ultracentrifugation by Beckman Coulter Life Sciences>[Rate Zonal Centrifugation: top loading, preformed gradient, starting-zone width and stopping before pelleting]{ref}<Density Gradient Centrifugation by Beckman Coulter Life Sciences>[Types of Density Gradients: rate-zonal preformed sucrose gradients]{ref}

The <sedimentation coefficient> $s=(m-\rho_fV)/f$ explains why separation is not an ordering by <mass> alone. At fixed local <centrifugal acceleration>, increasing displaced <volume> can increase <buoyancy>, while a less compact shape can increase the <linear friction coefficient> and reduce <velocity>. Even for a <sphere>, <Stokes's law> gives $s=2b^2(\rho_p-\rho_f)/(9\eta)$, so both the <density> difference and the dynamic <viscosity> matter. The resulting <sedimentation coefficient> can change along a <density gradient>.

= Finite-time rate-zonal separation
{parent=Rate-zonal centrifugation}

Consider two dilute populations of <spheres> with the same <buoyant density> and radii $b$ and $2b$. Adopt the fixed linear <density gradient> and constant local <centrifugal acceleration> of <linear-gradient isopycnic focusing>, with constant dynamic <viscosity> and negligible <diffusion>. <Stokes's law> makes $V/f$ proportional to $b^2$, so doubling radius multiplies the migration rate parameter by four, although the <mass> increases eightfold.

Use $u=z/L$, $\tau=t/T$ and $q=(\rho_b-\rho_0)/(GL)=2$. The liquid occupies $0\leq u\leq1$, so the matching <density> position lies beyond its outer boundary. Both populations begin at $u(0)=0.1$; choose $\lambda_A=0.15$ and $\lambda_B=0.6$. Their band-center trajectories are
$$
u_A(\tau)=2-1.9e^{-0.15\tau},\qquad u_B(\tau)=2-1.9e^{-0.6\tau}.
$$
At $\tau=0.5$, they lie at approximately $0.2373$ and $0.5924$; at $\tau=1$, at $0.3647$ and $0.9573$. Equal <buoyant density> has not prevented a useful finite-time separation. Solving $u_B(\tau)=1$ using the <natural logarithm> gives first boundary arrival at $\tau=\ln(1.9)/0.6\simeq1.0698$. The corresponding arrival for A is $\ln(1.9)/0.15\simeq4.2790$. Trajectories describe migration only until contact with the boundary; subsequent collection into a <centrifugation pellet> is not represented by extending the curve beyond $u=1$.

\Image[rate-zonal-bands-before-boundary-arrival.png]
{title=<Rate-zonal centrifugation>: equal <buoyant density>, different finite-time migration}
{description=Original schematic tubes and trajectories for the stipulated <finite-time rate-zonal separation> model. B represents <spheres> with twice A's radius. Both matching <density> positions lie outside the column. Tube shading shows an imposed increasing <density gradient>; band thickness is schematic. The marked boundary-arrival time is calculated from this model, not an experimental run time or an operating instruction.}

This is the useful contrast with <isopycnic centrifugation>: stopping time helps determine the recovered positions in <rate-zonal centrifugation>, whereas a stationary matching <density> position sets the limiting band center in <isopycnic centrifugation>. Real resolution also depends on initial zone width, <diffusion> and variations in particle properties; distinct calculated centers alone do not prove that fractions will be pure.

= Biophysics
{parent=Biology}

= Biophysicist
{parent=Biophysics}

= List of biophysicists
{parent=Biophysicist}

= W. Haase
{c}
{parent=List of biophysicists}

Publication record: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6334162/

= Work by W. Haase
{parent=W. Haase}

= Biophysical measurement
{parent=Biology}

= Förster resonance energy transfer
{parent=Biophysical measurement}
{c}

= FRET
{synonym}
{c}

<Förster resonance energy transfer> is a nonradiative transfer of <energy> from an <excited state> of a <FRET donor> to a nearby <FRET acceptor>, through coupling of their <electric dipoles>. The transfer step does not emit a <photon> that the <FRET acceptor> subsequently absorbs. It competes with the <FRET donor>'s own <fluorescence> and other routes out of its <excited state>. A fluorescent <FRET acceptor> can then emit <fluorescence>; an absorbing but nonfluorescent <FRET acceptor> can still receive the transferred <energy>.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.1: nonradiative dipole coupling and a potentially nonfluorescent acceptor]{ref}

The striking use of <FRET> is to turn a change in molecular proximity into an optical readout. <Proteins> carrying a <FRET donor> and a <FRET acceptor> may change the readout when they associate, as in the <chemotaxis FRET assay>; two labels on one <protein> may report a change in its shape. Interpretation depends on the <FRET spectral overlap>, <FRET dipole orientation factor> and labeling geometry, as well as separation. A change in <FRET> is therefore evidence about proximity under specified conditions, rather than a direct measurement of an arbitrary distance.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.1 and §3.3: dependence on distance and orientation; inter- and intramolecular applications]{ref}

For a single pair with fixed <Förster distance> $R_0$ and label separation $r$, the <FRET efficiency> is
$$
E=\frac{1}{1+(r/R_0)^6}.
$$
At $r=R_0$, half the <FRET donor> excitations transfer <energy>. At $r=R_0/2$, the model gives $E=64/65\approx98.46\%$; at $r=2R_0$, it gives $E=1/65\approx1.54\%$. The sixth power makes <FRET> especially useful near the <Förster distance>, with little sensitivity to further separation once transfer is already negligible. These are model calculations, not measurements of a particular pair.

\Image[forster-distance-transfer-efficiency.png]
{title=<FRET efficiency> versus separation relative to the <Förster distance>}
{description=Original single-pair <mathematical model> with fixed <Förster distance>. The shaded interval is $0.2\leq E\leq0.8$. The curve does not establish a universal working range or justify the <electric dipole> approximation for overlapping labels.}

= FRET donor
{parent=Förster resonance energy transfer}
{c}

A <FRET donor> supplies the initial <excited state> <energy>. Its <fluorescence> provides both an emission channel and a reference <FRET donor lifetime> when no <FRET acceptor> is present. Calling a label a <FRET donor> describes its role in a chosen pair, rather than a permanent classification of the <molecule>.

= FRET acceptor
{parent=Förster resonance energy transfer}
{c}

A <FRET acceptor> receives the <excited state> <energy>. Its absorption must support <FRET spectral overlap> with the <FRET donor>. <FRET sensitized emission> requires a fluorescent <FRET acceptor>; measuring a shortened <FRET donor lifetime> does not.

= FRET efficiency
{parent=Förster resonance energy transfer}
{c}
{title2=$E$}

<FRET efficiency> is the <probability> that an excitation of the <FRET donor> ends in transfer to the <FRET acceptor>. Write $k_D=k_{\rm r}+k_{\rm nr}$ for the total decay rate without a <FRET acceptor>, including radiative <fluorescence> and other decay, and $k_T$ for the transfer rate. For constant competing rates,
$$
k_T=k_D\left(\frac{R_0}{r}\right)^6,\qquad E=\frac{k_T}{k_D+k_T}=\int_0^\infty k_T e^{-(k_D+k_T)t}\,dt.
$$
The <integral> counts transfer events among all excitations in a population with <exponential decay>. Substitution gives the separation law above. This short rate argument assumes one <FRET donor>, one <FRET acceptor>, weak coupling, an available <FRET acceptor>, and fixed geometry during the decay; it is not a model for arbitrary interacting labels.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.1, equations 14–16 and discussion before §3.3: single-pair assumptions and transfer efficiency]{ref}

= Förster distance
{parent=FRET efficiency}
{c}
{title2=$R_0$}

= Förster radius
{synonym}
{c}

The <Förster distance> is the separation giving <FRET efficiency> $E=1/2$ for the specified pair and conditions. It is a property of the optical pair and its environment, rather than a universal length. In the usual pair model,
$$
R_0^6\propto \kappa^2 Q_D J n^{-4},
$$
where $\kappa^2$ is the <FRET dipole orientation factor>, $Q_D$ the <FRET donor quantum yield>, $J$ the <FRET spectral overlap> <integral>, and $n$ the medium's <refractive index>. The omitted proportionality constant depends on the unit convention for $J$.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.1, equations 16–18: factors controlling Förster distance]{ref}

If <FRET efficiency> is known and those assumptions hold, inversion gives
$$
r=R_0\left(E^{-1}-1\right)^{1/6},\qquad \frac{d\log r}{dE}=-\frac{1}{6E(1-E)}.
$$
The <derivative> explains why errors become troublesome near $E=0$ or $E=1$. An illustrative pair with $R_0=4$ nanometres and another with $R_0=6$ nanometres both give $E=1/2$ at their different <Förster distances>: knowing <FRET efficiency> alone does not determine $r$.

A heterogeneous population adds another ambiguity. In a synthetic example with equal weights per <FRET donor> excitation, efficiencies $0.1$ and $0.9$ average to $0.5$, but their separations are $9^{1/6}R_0$ and $9^{-1/6}R_0$. Their average separation is approximately $1.068R_0$, whereas inverting the average <FRET efficiency> gives $R_0$. A measured emission <ratio> has additional detection weights; it need not equal even this excitation-weighted average. The transferable lesson is that an average readout does not uniquely specify a distribution of molecular separations.

= FRET spectral overlap
{parent=Förster distance}
{c}
{title2=$J$}

<FRET spectral overlap> quantifies overlap between the <FRET donor>'s emission and the <FRET acceptor>'s absorption as functions of <wavelength> $\lambda$. With normalized donor emission $f_D$ and acceptor molar absorption coefficient $\varepsilon_A$, one convention is
$$
\int_0^\infty f_D(\lambda)\,d\lambda=1,\qquad J=\int_0^\infty f_D(\lambda)\varepsilon_A(\lambda)\lambda^4\,d\lambda.
$$
Matching two emission colors is insufficient: the receiving absorption matters.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.1, equation 17 and Figure 19: normalized donor emission and acceptor absorption overlap]{ref}

= FRET dipole orientation factor
{parent=Förster distance}
{c}
{title2=$\kappa^2$}

The <FRET dipole orientation factor> describes the geometry of the two transition <electric dipoles> relative to their separation. Let $\widehat{\boldsymbol\mu}_D$, $\widehat{\boldsymbol\mu}_A$ and $\widehat{\mathbf r}$ be their <unit vectors>. Then
$$
\kappa^2=\left[\widehat{\boldsymbol\mu}_D\cdot\widehat{\boldsymbol\mu}_A-3(\widehat{\boldsymbol\mu}_D\cdot\widehat{\mathbf r})(\widehat{\boldsymbol\mu}_A\cdot\widehat{\mathbf r})\right]^2,\qquad 0\leq\kappa^2\leq4.
$$
For a separation along $z$, parallel <electric dipoles> along $z$ give $\kappa^2=4$, parallel ones along $x$ give $1$, and one along $x$ with the other along $y$ gives $0$. Thus rotating a pair can change <FRET efficiency> without changing its separation.

The familiar value $\kappa^2=2/3$ requires suitable rapid, independent orientation averaging during the <FRET donor lifetime>. Random orientations across a population of slowly rotating fluorescent <proteins> do not establish that dynamic condition. <Quantitative time domain analysis of lifetime-based Förster resonant energy transfer measurements with fluorescent proteins: Static random isotropic fluorophore orientation distributions by Yuriy Alexandrov and others>{ref}

= FRET donor quantum yield
{parent=Förster distance}
{c}
{title2=$Q_D$}

The <FRET donor quantum yield> is the emitted <fluorescence> <photon> fraction per absorbed excitation <photon>, measured without a <FRET acceptor>. In the constant-rate model used for <FRET efficiency>, $Q_D=k_{\rm r}/(k_{\rm r}+k_{\rm nr})$. Changing the <FRET donor quantum yield> changes the <Förster distance>, even if label positions remain fixed.

= FRET donor lifetime
{parent=FRET efficiency}
{c}
{title2=$\tau_D,\tau_{DA}$}

The <FRET donor lifetime> is the mean time spent in the <excited state>. For the constant-rate model of <FRET efficiency>,
$$
\tau_D=\frac1{k_D},\qquad \tau_{DA}=\frac1{k_D+k_T},\qquad E=1-\frac{\tau_{DA}}{\tau_D}.
$$
After a sufficiently brief excitation pulse, the <FRET donor>'s <fluorescence> follows <exponential decay>, $I(t)=I(0)e^{-t/\tau}$. With the illustrative values $\tau_D=4$ nanoseconds and $\tau_{DA}=2$ nanoseconds, the inferred <FRET efficiency> is $1/2$. Multiplying $I(0)$ changes brightness without changing this decay time.

The lifetime comparison requires the same nontransfer decay rates in the reference and paired samples. Changes in the <FRET donor>'s environment or a different nonradiative loss can also shorten the <FRET donor lifetime>. Heterogeneous <FRET efficiencies> or slowly varying <FRET dipole orientation factors> need an appropriate decay model; substituting any fitted average lifetime into the single-pair formula can mislead. <Quantitative time domain analysis of lifetime-based Förster resonant energy transfer measurements with fluorescent proteins: Static random isotropic fluorophore orientation distributions by Yuriy Alexandrov and others>{ref}

\Image[fret-fluorescence-lifetime-readouts.png]
{title=<FRET sensitized emission>, optical controls and the <FRET donor lifetime>}
{description=Original schematic and synthetic curves. The spectral panel assumes equal <fluorescence> yields and collection efficiencies for the two labels; its amplitudes are illustrative, not a calibration or measured cyan/yellow spectra. The lifetime panel uses $\tau_D=4$ and $\tau_{DA}=2$ nanoseconds. Dashed optical paths require <FRET optical crosstalk> controls; they do not represent the nonradiative transfer mechanism.}

= FRET sensitized emission
{parent=Förster resonance energy transfer}
{c}

<FRET sensitized emission> is <FRET acceptor> <fluorescence> caused by transfer from an excited <FRET donor>. Excite predominantly the <FRET donor> and collect both emission bands. Reduced <FRET donor> emission and increased <FRET acceptor> emission are useful together, but an acceptor-to-donor emission <ratio> is not automatically the <FRET efficiency>: brightness, label abundance and channel collection also enter.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.4.2: sensitized emission and dependence on optical correction and label concentrations]{ref}

= FRET optical crosstalk
{parent=FRET sensitized emission}
{c}

<FRET optical crosstalk> includes <FRET donor> emission leaking into the <FRET acceptor> detection band and direct excitation of the <FRET acceptor> by the nominal <FRET donor> excitation light. Measure donor-only, acceptor-only and unlabeled preparations with the same <fluorescence microscope> settings to separate these contributions and background.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.4.2, samples i–iii and equations 20–21: spectral correction controls]{ref}

An explicit linear measurement model makes the controls' purpose clear. Let $D$ be background-subtracted donor-band counts under donor excitation, $A$ acceptor-band counts under acceptor excitation, and $F$ acceptor-band counts under donor excitation. Donor-only and acceptor-only preparations determine leakage coefficients $a$ and $b$. Then
$$
S=F-aD-bA
$$
is a corrected <FRET sensitized emission> signal under the assumed linear, fixed optical response. In a synthetic example $D=100$, $A=80$, $a=0.10$, $b=0.05$ and $F=34$ give $S=20$. Even a nontransferring sample would give $F=14$ from these two contaminants. Correcting that signal still does not convert $S$ into <FRET efficiency> without a calibration of excitation, yields and detection.

A biological comparison also needs a pairing-negative preparation and a known transferring positive preparation. Match label abundance and localization where possible; changing <concentration> can change encounter frequency as well as brightness. Check that attaching the labels preserves the <protein> function being investigated. Otherwise the measurement can faithfully report a perturbation introduced by the labels themselves.

= FRET photobleaching controls
{parent=Förster resonance energy transfer}
{c}

In <FRET photobleaching controls>, selectively destroy the <FRET acceptor>'s ability to absorb and test whether <FRET donor> <fluorescence> increases. Under unchanged excitation and detection, complete loss of transfer, and an intact <FRET donor>,
$$
E=1-\frac{I_{D,\rm before}}{I_{D,\rm after}}.
$$
This is a destructive comparison. Monitor acceptor loss, donor-only samples exposed to the bleaching illumination, and an unbleached reference region or time trace. Incomplete bleaching, donor damage, movement or label replenishment violate the comparison. During ordinary recordings, gradual unequal bleaching can instead produce an apparent change of <FRET> without a biological change.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.4.1.1–3.4.1.2: donor recovery, sample damage, acceptor replenishment and photoconversion artifacts]{ref}

= FRET proximity readout experiment
{parent=Förster resonance energy transfer}
{c}

In <Receptor sensitivity in bacterial chemotaxis by Victor Sourjik and others>, <Victor Sourjik> and <Howard C. Berg> used a cyan fluorescent tag on <CheZ> as <FRET donor> and a yellow tag on <CheY> as <FRET acceptor>. <Escherichia coli> <biological cells> adhered to a polylysine-coated coverslip in a continuously perfused chamber. A <fluorescence microscope> excited them with a $442$-nanometre <laser>; split emission bands fed two <photon>-counting detectors. The yellow/cyan emission <ratio> fell when attractant was added and rose when it was removed.<Receptor sensitivity in bacterial chemotaxis by Victor Sourjik and others>[Introduction, Materials and Methods: Preparation of Cells and Data Acquisition; Results and Figure 1]{ref}

Saturating attractant increased cyan emission by an amount matching selective bleaching of the yellow tag: removing the <FRET acceptor> supported relief of <FRET donor> transfer. Strains carrying the tagged pair but lacking other <chemotaxis> machinery showed no stimulus-dependent emission <ratio> change. Weak illumination reduced bleaching drift.<Receptor sensitivity in bacterial chemotaxis by Victor Sourjik and others>[Data Analysis and Results: acceptor bleaching comparison, low illumination, and flhC negative control]{ref}

The inference is a change in the population of nearby tagged <CheY>/<CheZ> pairs associated with <CheY> <phosphorylation>. It neither measures each pair's separation nor directly observes motor motion. The readout also averages many <biological cells>, and perfusion takes finite time. The pathway interpretation belongs to the <chemotaxis FRET assay>; the general measurement lesson is that molecular association can alter <FRET> while the geometry of an individual associated pair stays the same.

= Model organism
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

A <model organism> is an <organism> used to investigate biological processes through accessible, repeatable experiments. Its value combines <experimental tractability> with a carefully delimited relationship to other <organisms>. For example, a <gene deletion> in <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> can test whether a <gene> is required for growth under specified conditions; adding another <gene> can test <functional complementation>. Such interventions support <causal inference> more directly than merely noticing that a <gene> and a <phenotype> occur together. <Budding Yeast for Budding Geneticists: A Primer on the Saccharomyces cerevisiae Model System by Andrea A. Duina and others>[Duina, Miller and Keeney (2014), introduction and Experimental Toolkit and Related Resources: manipulation of genes and phenotypic effects]{ref}

The striking opportunity is to ask two experimentally separate questions: what does this component do here, and which part of that function survives a change of <species> or conditions? The <yeast cross-species complementation experiment> makes both questions concrete. <Model organism evidence transfer> requires specifying the function being compared; a useful <model organism> need not reproduce every feature of another <organism>.

= Experimental tractability
{parent=Model organism}

<Experimental tractability> is the practical ability to cultivate a system, manipulate the variables of interest, and obtain an interpretable readout with appropriate <scientific controls>. In <Saccharomyces cerevisiae>, inexpensive cultivation, accessible <haploid> and <diploid> states, and tools for introducing <DNA> make controlled changes of <genotype> feasible. A <haploid> <biological strain> exposes a changed <allele> without a second copy at that <genetic locus>, while crosses and <meiosis> permit tests of inheritance. <Budding Yeast for Budding Geneticists: A Primer on the Saccharomyces cerevisiae Model System by Andrea A. Duina and others>[Duina, Miller and Keeney (2014), introduction, Figure 2 and Experimental Toolkit and Related Resources]{ref}

An accessible readout is not necessarily a direct measurement of the mechanism. Growth of a <biological colony> integrates many cellular functions. Failure to grow could reflect the intended <gene deletion>, unsuitable conditions, failed <genetic transformation>, or another <mutation>. Matching the <genetic background> and using <scientific controls> narrows these alternatives. Ease of doing an experiment and strength of its inference are distinct advantages.

= Yeast cross-species complementation experiment
{parent=Model organism}

Can a <gene> from <Schizosaccharomyces pombe> replace a missing function in <Saccharomyces cerevisiae>? A modern laboratory project tested <functional complementation> of <methionine> synthesis. For each tested <gene deletion>, the investigators compared <plasmids> carrying the corresponding <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> <gene>, a candidate <Schizosaccharomyces pombe> <gene>, and an unrelated <gene>. The first was a positive <scientific control>; the last was a negative <scientific control>. The paper describes this design rather than publishing a complete set of individual growth measurements. <Pathways over Time: Functional Genomics Research in an Introductory Laboratory Course by Todd D. Reeves and others>[Reeves, Warner, Ludlow and O’Connor (2018), Course Activities, pp. 5–6 and Table 1: three plasmids, replica plating and protein-expression checks]{ref}

The recipient <biological strains> also lacked the ability to make <uracil>. The pYES2.1/V5-His <plasmids> carried URA3, a <gene> restoring that ability, and used the GAL1 <gene promoter> to control the inserted <gene>. Initial plates lacked <uracil> but supplied <methionine>: this selected <biological colonies> containing <plasmids> without requiring successful <methionine> synthesis. Subsequent plates lacking <methionine> tested the missing function, comparing <galactose induction> with <glucose> conditions. Thus selecting for a <plasmid> and testing its functional contribution were separate operations. <Pathways Over Time Plasmids Engage Students in Functional Genomics Research by Clare O’Connor>[O’Connor (2018), Plasmid features enable multiple student experiments and Complementation reveals conservation of function]{ref}

The apparatus can be simple and informative. In the associated protocol, <replica plating> uses a block covered with velveteen to transfer small samples of <biological colonies> from a master plate onto plates with different nutrient conditions. Orientation marks preserve correspondence between locations; gentle contact limits the transferred material. The specified replicas omit <uracil> and <methionine> with either inducing <sugar> or <glucose>, and include a fresh <glucose> plate supplying <methionine>. The readout is growth at corresponding locations, not a direct measurement of <enzyme> activity. <Investigations in Molecular Cell Biology by Clare M. O’Connor/12.6: Exercise 2 - Replica plating and complementation>[O’Connor, Investigations in Molecular Cell Biology, §12.6, introductory paragraphs and steps 1–3: velveteen block, orientation marks and three replica media]{ref}

\Image[yeast-gene-complementation-experimental-design.png]
{title=Separating <plasmid> selection from a <yeast> <functional complementation> test}
{description=Original schematic of the reported experimental design, not copied growth data. All three constructs enter the same <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> <gene deletion> background. Plate labels identify distinct comparisons; arrows show sample transfer, not observed rescue. The candidate <Schizosaccharomyces pombe> outcome must be measured.}

The <scientific controls> answer different objections. Growth on the <methionine>-supplied replica checks survival and transfer. Rescue by the native <gene> checks that this <biological strain> and assay can reveal <functional complementation>. The unrelated <gene> tests whether carrying a <plasmid> alone is sufficient. Comparing induction conditions checks dependence on <gene expression>, although changing the <sugar> also changes <metabolism>: it is not an isolated change of <gene expression> in every other respect.

If the candidate <gene> restores growth while those comparisons behave as intended, it supplies a function sufficient for the tested growth requirement in this recipient. Failure to rescue is less decisive: the candidate <protein> might not be produced, might be altered by its attached tag, or might not work in this cellular context. The investigators therefore also used <Western blots> to examine <protein> production. A detected <protein> still does not establish correct <protein folding> or catalytic activity. <Pathways over Time: Functional Genomics Research in an Introductory Laboratory Course by Todd D. Reeves and others>[Reeves et al. (2018), Course Activities, pp. 5–6: functional conservation, tag interference and Western-blot follow-up]{ref}

= Model organism evidence transfer
{parent=Model organism}

Evidence transfers at a stated level. <Sequence alignment> can suggest a candidate relationship between <genes>; <functional complementation> tests whether a candidate can supply a particular function in a chosen recipient. Neither alone establishes identical native <gene expression>, identical <signal transduction>, or identical consequences in every <species>. The <yeast cross-species complementation experiment> is especially useful because it tests a conserved function by intervention rather than assuming it from resemblance. <Pathways over Time: Functional Genomics Research in an Introductory Laboratory Course by Todd D. Reeves and others>[Reeves et al. (2018), project design, pp. 3–4, and Course Activities, pp. 5–6]{ref}

\Image[model-organism-evidence-transfer-boundaries.png]
{title=What a <model organism> experiment establishes, and what needs another test}
{description=Original evidence map using the <yeast cross-species complementation experiment>. Solid arrows indicate an experimental question that can be addressed in the recipient; dashed arrows mark additional claims requiring evidence. This is a schematic of inference boundaries, not a <phylogenetic tree>, a measured pathway, or a claim about medical outcomes.}

Even successful <functional complementation> occurs within a specific <genetic background>, nutrient supply and imposed <gene expression>. The <enzyme> may work with the recipient’s available <substrates> and partner <proteins> at an experimentally imposed abundance. Establishing the same role in the donor <species> calls for experiments there; establishing behavior under another condition calls for experiments under that condition. A conserved component can therefore coexist with context-dependent behavior of an entire pathway. This separation makes <model organisms> powerful: an accessible experiment supplies a precise mechanistic foothold and a better question for the next system.

\Include[organism]{parent=Biology}

= Taxonomy
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Taxonomy
{synonym}

<Taxonomy> names and classifies <organisms>. Classification can organize known diversity and make comparisons useful, but ranks and names are not themselves evidence of ancestry. <Phylogenetics> infers that ancestry; a <clade> includes an ancestor and all its descendants. A shared feature such as lacking a <cell nucleus> need not identify an exclusive <clade>. The <organism> branch here organizes cell types for reading and is not a proposed <phylogenetic tree>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/20-1-organizing-life-on-earth[OpenStax Biology 2e, 20.1: classification and evolutionary history].

= Domain
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Taxonomy (biology)}
{wiki}

A biological <domain (biology)> is a high-level rank used to classify <organisms> in the <three-domain system>. Distinguish a taxonomic rank from a primary branch in an inferred <phylogenetic tree>: retaining familiar names does not settle the branching relationships.

= Three-domain system
{parent=Domain (biology)}
{wiki}

The <three-domain system> recognizes <bacteria>, <archaea> and <eukaryotes> as three domains. In 1990, Woese, Kandler and Wheelis proposed the domain rank and the names Bacteria, Archaea and Eucarya, using molecular comparisons to argue that the older <prokaryote>/<eukaryote> division hid important differences. See https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2112744/[Woese, Kandler and Wheelis, 1990: Towards a natural system of organisms].

The associated three-domain ancestry model places the nuclear lineage of <eukaryotes> alongside <archaea>, rather than within them. This historically influential interpretation should be distinguished from the <two-domain system>; listing three familiar groups does not establish three independent primary branches.

= Two-domain system
{parent=Domain (biology)}
{wiki}

The <two-domain system> recognizes <bacteria> and <archaea> as the two primary lineages, placing the nuclear ancestry of <eukaryotes> within <archaea>. In this model, <archaea> as a complete <clade> includes that <eukaryotic> lineage; restricting the name to <prokaryotic> members describes a different grouping. Williams and colleagues' study, published online in 2019 and in a 2020 journal issue, found support for this model and a close relationship to <Asgard archaea>. See https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-019-1040-x[Williams et al., 2020: Phylogenomics provides robust support for a two-domains tree of life].

This updates the interpretation of the 1977 and 1990 classifications rather than undoing their discovery of deep differences between <bacteria> and <archaea>. Deep <phylogenetic trees> depend on the sampled <genes>, <organisms> and inference model; the precise closest archaeal relatives and early sequence of events require further evidence. Bacterial ancestry of <mitochondria> through <endosymbiosis> is a separate part of <eukaryotic> history.

= Species
{parent=Taxonomy (biology)}
{wiki}

A <species> is a named unit of biological classification. The useful criteria vary among groups: reproductive compatibility can inform classification in many <animals>, while microbial comparisons also use <genomes> and other characters. <Escherichia coli> is a <species>; K-12 MG1655 identifies a particular strain within it. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/18-2-formation-of-new-species[OpenStax Biology 2e, 18.2: species concepts].

= Species concept
{parent=Species}

A <species concept> states what should make <organisms> members of the same <species>; an operational criterion states what observations will test that claim. <Reproductive isolation>, distinguishable <morphology> and ancestry inferred through <phylogenetics> answer different questions. In a lineage-based interpretation, they are evidence for separation of evolving <biological populations>, and need not appear together. <Species Concepts and Species Delimitation by Kevin de Queiroz>[pp. 880–883, Table 1, Figure 1 and “Conceptualization versus Delimitation”: distinguish defining properties from operational evidence]{ref}

= Biological species concept
{parent=Species concept}

The <biological species concept> groups naturally interbreeding <biological populations> with viable, fertile offspring, distinguished from other such groups by <reproductive isolation>. An operational test examines mating, <fertilization>, offspring survival and offspring <reproduction>, rather than only whether two adults can be paired. <Sexual reproduction> is its scope: <fossils> and <organisms> studied only through <asexual reproduction> cannot supply the same mating evidence. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[§18.2, “Species and the Ability to Reproduce”, “Speciation” and “Reproductive Isolation”]{ref}

For an original hypothetical example, imagine two separated <biological populations> that reproduce successfully after contact. The separation alone did not demonstrate <reproductive isolation>. Conversely, failure to mate in one unfamiliar chamber could reflect the assay conditions. Field observations of <gene flow> and tests under suitable conditions address the biological question more directly. A successful laboratory pairing does not establish frequent interbreeding in nature.

= Morphological species concept
{parent=Species concept}

A <morphological species concept> distinguishes <species> using <morphology>: measurable differences in form or structure. It can be applied to preserved specimens and <fossils> when mating cannot be observed. Similar appearance need not imply the same <species>, and different appearance need not imply different <species>. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[§18.2, “Species and the Ability to Reproduce”, paragraphs accompanying Figures 18.9–18.10: appearance can mislead]{ref}

An original operational example is to compare a chosen structure in specimens of comparable age, sex and rearing conditions. Suppose two apparent groups differ in size, but one group contains only juveniles. The observed difference does not yet diagnose two <species>. Specify the measured <phenotype>, variation within each sample, and whether the distinction persists across comparable specimens. A single striking specimen is a poor substitute for that comparison.

= Phylogenetic species concept
{parent=Species concept}

<Phylogenetic species concepts> use ancestry and diagnostic inherited differences; versions differ over requiring a <clade>, an exclusive ancestry pattern at <genetic loci>, or a distinguishable character combination. State which criterion is intended. <Species Concepts and Species Delimitation by Kevin de Queiroz>[p. 880, Table 1: distinct monophyletic, genealogical and diagnosable versions of phylogenetic criteria]{ref}

For an original operational example, suppose a <phylogenetic tree> inferred from one <genetic locus> separates two sampled <biological populations>. Compare further <genetic loci> and additional local samples before interpreting that branch as a <species> boundary. Otherwise the test may merely identify the particular ancestry of the sampled <alleles>. Adding more sites from the same <genetic locus> improves that locus's evidence without creating an independent ancestry test.

= Species delimitation
{parent=Species}

<Species delimitation> infers the boundaries and number of <species> from evidence. No single arbitrary percentage of <nucleotide sequence> difference is a universal boundary. Divergence within an old, widespread <species> can exceed divergence between recently separated <species>; geography and multiple kinds of evidence matter. <Species Concepts and Species Delimitation by Kevin de Queiroz>[pp. 884–885, “Alternatives to the Traditional Properties”: divergence cutoffs and geographic differentiation]{ref}

An original counterexample to a universal percentage rule: an assay could examine a strongly conserved <gene> in one comparison and a rapidly changing <nucleotide sequence> in another. Equal percentages then answer different historical questions. Even the denominator needs specifying: all sites, aligned sites, or only a filtered region? A threshold is an operational rule for a stated assay and group of <organisms>, whose errors need evaluating. It is not a definition that automatically transfers to every <species>.

\Image[species-evidence-criteria.png]
{title=Contrasting operational evidence for <species delimitation>}
{description=Original hypothetical comparison of <morphology>, mating and ancestry evidence. Each observation tests a different claim; the combinations are thought experiments, not a dataset or verdicts about named <species>. “Not tested” does not mean absent.}

The diagram's hypothetical pair B has similar <morphology> but reduced mating and distinct ancestry evidence. A <morphological species concept> could miss that distinction. Pair C instead has different <morphology> but compatible mating and ancestry that fails to separate the samples. That combination calls for checking variation in <phenotype> within a <species>. Pair A has only a structural difference observed: the other criteria remain untested. These examples show why missing evidence, disagreeing evidence and agreeing evidence require different interpretations.

= Species, populations and strains
{parent=Species}

A <species> can encompass many <biological populations>. A <biological population> specifies a set of <organisms> in a place and time; a <biological strain> identifies a maintained variant or lineage, often in a laboratory. A <species> name, a local census and a <biological strain> designation therefore describe different things. Multiple independently maintained cultures can also share the same <biological strain> designation.

For example, a culture identified as <Escherichia coli> K-12 MG1655 specifies more than the <species> alone. Two flasks inoculated from that culture are separate experimental <biological populations>; the separate containers do not establish two <species> or two different <biological strains>. This is an original experimental bookkeeping example, not a claim that every <biological strain> name encodes a complete <genotype>.

= Morphology
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Morphology
{synonym}

<Morphology> concerns biological form and structure; a structural difference is one aspect of <phenotype>, not automatically a <species> boundary.

= Developmental biology
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Biological development
{parent=Developmental biology}

= Blastula
{parent=Biological development}
{wiki}

A <blastula> is an early multicellular stage of <animal> <biological development>. The <intestinal nucleus transfer experiment> distinguishes development beyond this stage from the earliest divisions.

= Paleontology
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Fossil
{parent=Paleontology}
{wiki}

= Ecology
{parent=Biology}

The goal of <ecology> is to explain where <organisms> live, how many there are, and how their interactions with one another and their surroundings change those patterns. Living partners include <prey>, <predators> and <parasites>; nonliving conditions include <light>, <temperature> and <water>. An <ecosystem> brings these interacting parts into one study system. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/44-1-the-scope-of-ecology[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 44.1: the scope of ecology].

Start with an invented aquatic <food web>: photosynthetic <algae> feed <zooplankton>, which feed small <fish>, which feed large <fish>. That <food chain> has four feeding positions and three feeding arrows. Dead material also reaches <decomposers> through <detritus>, so the <food web> branches rather than forming a single ladder.

\Image[illustrative-food-web.png]
{title=An illustrative aquatic <food web>: feeding arrows point from resource to <consumer>}
{description=Original schematic, not a measured <ecosystem>. Solid arrows mean RESOURCE → CONSUMER; dashed arrows mean transfer of dead material to <detritus>. Box sizes and arrow widths do not represent abundance or transfer rates. <Algae>, <zooplankton>, small <fish> and large <fish> are generic groups, not identified <species>. Selected dead-material transfers are shown; further feeding and recycling routes are omitted.}

The appeal of <ecology> is that an <organism>'s surroundings include other <organisms> that change those surroundings. <Photosynthesis> links <light> to living material; <predation> transfers that material between <organisms>; <decomposers> return its constituent <chemical elements> to forms that can be used again. These connections help explain why changing one part of an <ecosystem> can affect distant parts of its <food web>, although an arrow alone does not predict the size or even the direction of an indirect change. <Population dynamics> supplies quantitative tools when the question concerns changing counts.

In 1866, Ernst Haeckel's \i[Generelle Morphologie der Organismen] included a section titled \i[Oecologie und Chorologie] in volume 2, beginning on page 286. This places the terminology of <ecology> in an early systematic treatment of <organisms> and their relationships. See https://books.google.com/books?id=tKFbIVgJS8wC[Haeckel, Generelle Morphologie der Organismen, volume 2 (1866), contents and p. 286]. A later quantitative landmark is Raymond L. Lindeman's 1942 treatment of <ecosystems> through feeding relationships and <energy flow>. See https://doi.org/10.2307/1930126[Lindeman, The Trophic-Dynamic Aspect of Ecology, Ecology 23 (1942), 399–417].

= Population
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Ecology}

= Biological population
{synonym}

A <biological population> is a set of <organisms> of a <species> occupying a specified area or experimental setting at a specified time. State the boundary for the question being studied: a flask, a breeding locality, or a wider region connected by <gene flow>. A <biological population> need not be perfectly mixed or isolated.

= Ecosystem
{parent=Ecology}
{wiki}

An <ecosystem> is a system of interacting <organisms> and their nonliving surroundings. A pond study might include <algae>, <fish>, <bacteria>, <water>, dissolved <chemical compounds> and incoming <light>. The useful step is to state the boundary and time interval: the pond surface, bottom and shoreline might enclose the study region, but <water>, <organisms> and <energy> can cross them. The chosen boundary specifies what counts as internal storage, input or output; it does not make the <ecosystem> sealed. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-1-ecology-of-ecosystems[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.1: ecosystems and their components].

For a toy pond <water> budget over one day, begin with $1000\,\mathrm{m^3}$, add $120\,\mathrm{m^3}$ crossing inward, and remove $90\,\mathrm{m^3}$ crossing outward. With all other transfers omitted by assumption, the final amount is $1000+120-90=1030\,\mathrm{m^3}$. The same bookkeeping asks whether a change inside an <ecosystem> came from internal processes or exchanges with its surroundings. Changing the boundary to include an upstream reservoir changes which transfers are internal, while preserving the accounting.

= Primary producer
{parent=Ecosystem}

A <primary producer> builds organic <chemical compounds> from inorganic sources. Photosynthetic <algae>, <plants> and <cyanobacteria> use <light> <energy> for this role through <photosynthesis> and <carbon fixation>. The role supplies new living material to a <food web>; it does not mean that the <organism> creates <energy>.

= Consumer
{disambiguate=food chain}
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}

= Consumer
{synonym}

A <consumer> obtains organic material by feeding on other <organisms> or their products. A <consumer> feeding on <primary producers> is a primary <consumer>; one feeding on primary <consumers> is a secondary <consumer>. These names describe positions in a <food chain>, rather than permanent labels for a <species>.

= Decomposer
{parent=Consumer (food chain)}
{wiki}

A <decomposer> breaks down dead organic material and absorbs products of that breakdown; many <bacteria> and <fungi> perform this role. <Decomposers> connect <detritus> from different <trophic levels> to material recycling, so placing all <decomposers> at one final step of every <food chain> is misleading. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-1-ecology-of-ecosystems[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.1: grazing and detrital food webs].

= Omnivore
{parent=Consumer (food chain)}
{wiki}

An <omnivore> feeds on both <primary producers> and <consumers>. Its mixed diet explains why a <trophic level> need not be a whole number or a fixed label throughout an <organism>'s life.

= Detritus
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}

<Detritus> is nonliving organic material, including dead remains and organic wastes. It is a resource for <decomposers>, not itself an <organism> or a <predator>. In the illustrative <food web>, the dashed arrow from <algae> to <detritus> denotes a material transfer; the solid arrow from <detritus> to <decomposers> denotes resource use.

= Trophic level
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{TL}$}

A <trophic level> describes feeding position relative to <primary producers>. Assign <primary producers> level 1; in the illustrated <food chain>, <zooplankton> occupy level 2, small <fish> level 3, and large <fish> level 4. Following a single feeding path adds one level at each step. The labels make dependence on the initial resource visible, but a branching <food web> can give an <organism> several feeding paths. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-1-ecology-of-ecosystems[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.1: feeding positions and branching food webs].

An <omnivore> can obtain food from more than one <trophic level>. As an explicitly assumed diet-weighted example, suppose an <omnivore> gets half its assimilated food <energy> from level-1 <primary producers> and half from level-2 <consumers>. Define its <trophic level> as one plus the weighted <mean> level of its food: $1+(1/2)1+(1/2)2=2.5$. This computed position depends on the specified diet and weighting convention; it is not an immutable property of the <species>. A different life stage or diet can change it.

= Energy flow
{disambiguate=ecology}
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}

= Energy flow
{synonym}

<Energy flow> tracks <energy> entering an <ecosystem>, passing through <organisms> and leaving its boundary. To see its quantitative effect, assume net production at one <trophic level> is $P_1=1000\,\mathrm{kJ\,m^{-2}\,day^{-1}}$ and assume a transfer efficiency $\varepsilon=0.10$ at each of two successive steps. Here $P_i$ measures the <energy> incorporated into new living material per unit <area> per day, after that level's own <cellular respiration>; it is not a count of <organisms> or a standing stock. The <joule> supplies the <energy> unit, with $1\,\mathrm{kJ}=1000\,\mathrm J$.
$$
P_{i+1}=\varepsilon P_i,\qquad P_2=100,\qquad P_3=10\quad\mathrm{kJ\,m^{-2}\,day^{-1}}.
$$

\Image[trophic-energy-budget.png]
{title=An assumed <energy flow> budget: 1000 → 100 → 10 kJ per square metre per day}
{description=Original toy calculation with 10 percent transfer efficiency assumed at both steps. Bars use one linear scale; the labels retain visibility of the smallest bar. The differences 900 and 90 are production not transferred along the selected <food chain>, not measurements of <heat> alone. The example is separate from the four-position illustrative <food web>.}

Only $10/1000=0.01$, or one percent, of the initial production reaches level 3 in this toy <food chain>. The first step leaves $1000-100=900$ and the second $100-10=90$ outside the next level's new production. These differences can include uneaten material, wastes, and <energy> released as <heat> during <cellular respiration>; their separate amounts have not been assigned. <Detritus> can still carry <energy> to <decomposers>. The 10 percent efficiency is an assumption, not a universal law: transfer efficiencies vary with the <organisms>, food and conditions. The production ratio $P_{i+1}/P_i$ also differs from the fraction of an individual meal converted into growth. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-2-energy-flow-through-ecosystems[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.2: production and transfer efficiency].

The shrinking budget explains why supporting another <trophic level> requires enough production below it. It does not prove an exact maximum length for every <food chain>, particularly when a <food web> imports resources or has branching diets. Lindeman's 1942 paper grouped feeding relationships into <trophic levels>, compared transfers and <cellular respiration>, and emphasized the <ecosystem> as a unit connecting living and nonliving components. That viewpoint made different <organisms> comparable through a common <energy> budget. See https://doi.org/10.2307/1930126[Lindeman, The Trophic-Dynamic Aspect of Ecology (1942)].

Material recycling and <energy flow> describe different bookkeeping. <Carbon>, <nitrogen> and <phosphorus> can be reused through <chemical reactions> and transfers between <organisms> and their surroundings. <Energy> transferred as <heat> is conserved, but is no longer the same store of chemical <energy> available to a <consumer>; photosynthetic <primary producers> need continuing <light> input. A <decomposer> helps recycle material and also releases <heat> through <metabolism>. Thus loss from one feeding budget means transfer into other forms or routes, never destruction of <energy>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-3-biogeochemical-cycles[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.3: material cycles]. <Conservation of energy> applies to the whole accounting.

= Zooplankton
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}

<Zooplankton> are drifting aquatic <consumers>, including small <animals>. In the illustrative <food web>, the chosen <zooplankton> feed on <algae> and serve as <prey> for small <fish>; this assumed diet is not a claim about every kind of <zooplankton>.

= Endosymbiosis
{parent=Ecology}

<Endosymbiosis> is an ecological association in which one <organism>, the <endosymbiont>, lives inside another. The definition specifies location, not that both partners benefit, nor that the internal partner has become an <organelle>. An intracellular association can remain an association between distinct <organisms>. <Endosymbiotic theory> addresses a different question: how particular ancestral <bacteria> became inherited, functionally integrated <organelles> during <evolution>.

The striking consequence is that a <eukaryotic> <biological cell> can combine histories: its nuclear lineage and its <mitochondria> need not have the same ancestry. <Mitochondria> have bacterial ancestry related to <Alphaproteobacteria>; the <plastids> of <plants> and many <algae>, including <chloroplasts>, have ancestry within <cyanobacteria>. These are ancestral lineages, not a claim that a named living bacterial <species> is the literal ancestor. Present-day <bacteria> and <organelles> have both evolved since their shared ancestors. <Origin and Evolution of Plastids and Photosynthesis in Eukaryotes by Geoffrey I. McFadden>[Opening paragraphs and “The endosymbiont: when and who?”: bacterial origins and uncertainty about closest relatives]{ref}

\Image[endosymbiosis-organelle-integration.png]
{title=From an internal partner to an integrated <organelle>}
{description=Original conceptual schematic, not an observed historical sequence. Persistence, metabolic exchange, <endosymbiotic gene transfer>, <organelle protein import> and coordination of division illustrate integration; they are not dated stages in a compulsory order. The illustrated partner is generic, and the host outline does not specify the ancestral host of <mitochondria>. The protein-delivery arrow abbreviates nuclear <gene expression>, <biological translation> in the <cytosol> and <organelle protein import>; it does not place <protein> synthesis inside the <cell nucleus>.}

= Endosymbiont
{parent=Endosymbiosis}

An <endosymbiont> is the internal partner in an <endosymbiosis>. Calling an <organism> an <endosymbiont> does not by itself classify it as an <organelle> or imply an irreversible evolutionary transition.

= Endosymbiotic theory
{parent=Endosymbiosis}

<Endosymbiotic theory> explains the bacterial origins of <mitochondria> and <plastids> through enduring internal associations and subsequent integration. Survival inside a <biological cell> is only part of the problem: exchange through <membrane transport>, inherited division, <gene expression> and delivery of required <proteins> must work together. The elegance is evolutionary reuse of an existing cellular capability, followed by changes that make the partners operate as one system. <Origin and Evolution of Plastids and Photosynthesis in Eukaryotes by Geoffrey I. McFadden>[Abstract and “How to tame your symbiont”: metabolic, genetic and division integration]{ref}

The historical <On the origin of mitosing cells by Lynn Sagan> (1967, abstract and Introduction, pp. 225–226) synthesized a symbiotic account of <eukaryotic> origins and proposed tests using molecular evidence. Its Introduction explicitly acknowledged earlier proposals. It also hypothesized origins for other structures; the modern evidence for <mitochondria> and <plastids> should be assessed separately from the rest of that historical scheme.

= Evidence for bacterial ancestry of organelles
{parent=Endosymbiotic theory}

Resemblance is a clue, not sufficient evidence of <common descent>. A small size, two surrounding membranes, division, retained <DNA> or bacterial-looking <ribosomes> does not uniquely identify an ancestor. Internal membranes exist outside these <organelles>, and a similar function can favor similar structures. The stronger question is whether independently examined molecular characters support the same historical relationship.

A concrete genomic observation is the mitochondrial sequence reported in 1997 for <Reclinomonas americana>: $69{,}034$ <base pairs> containing 97 identified <genes>, including four for a bacterial-type multisubunit <RNA polymerase>. This is a reported <genome> inventory, not a calculation from the pictured shape of a <mitochondrion>. Retention of such bacterial features strengthens the ancestry argument; a living <Reclinomonas americana> is not itself an ancestral <eukaryote>. The inspected abstract supplies these observations but not the detailed sequencing protocol or contamination controls, so those details are not attributed to this report here. <An ancestral mitochondrial DNA resembling a eubacterial genome in miniature by B. Franz Lang and others>[Abstract, Nature 387 (1997), pp. 493–497: genome length, gene inventory and bacterial-type RNA polymerase]{ref}

A sequence-based investigation obtains <nucleotide sequences> by <DNA sequencing>, identifies comparable <genes>, makes a <sequence alignment> and uses <phylogenetics> to infer relationships. The readout is a proposed relationship with support and uncertainty, not a photograph of an ancient encounter. Comparisons of <plastid> sequences with bacterial sequences support cyanobacterial ancestry, while the exact closest sampled cyanobacterial group has depended on the chosen data and inference. A bacterial affinity in one <gene> is weaker than coherent evidence across several <genes> and cellular systems. <Origin and Evolution of Plastids and Photosynthesis in Eukaryotes by Geoffrey I. McFadden>[“The endosymbiont: when and who?”: alternative phylogenetic placements and lateral gene transfer]{ref}

For such an analysis, useful checks include distinguishing <organelle> <DNA> from bacterial contamination, verifying sequence quality, comparing corresponding rather than unrelated <genes>, sampling several bacterial groups and testing sensitivity to the <sequence alignment> and evolutionary <mathematical model>. These are methodological requirements, not a claim that this article has rerun a particular published analysis. <Horizontal gene transfer>, <gene> loss and unequal evolutionary change can make an individual <gene>'s history differ from a whole <organism>'s history. A supported bacterial origin therefore need not identify an extant donor <species>, an exact date or the original mode of entry. <Origin and Evolution of Plastids and Photosynthesis in Eukaryotes by Geoffrey I. McFadden>[“The endosymbiont: when and who?”: ancient origin and confounding lateral transfer]{ref}

= Endosymbiotic gene transfer
{parent=Endosymbiotic theory}

<Endosymbiotic gene transfer> moves genetic material from an <endosymbiont> or its descendant <organelle> into the host <genome>, notably the nuclear <genome>. A transferred <DNA> segment can remain silent. For a transferred <gene> to replace an <organelle>-encoded function, nuclear <gene expression> must work and its <protein> must reach the right compartment through <organelle protein import>. Transfer of <DNA> and transport of <proteins> are distinct processes in opposite directions. <Experimental Reconstruction of Functional Gene Transfer from the Tobacco Plastid Genome to the Nucleus by Sandra Stegemann and Ralph Bock>[Introduction, p. 2869, and Discussion, p. 2875: transferred sequences and the additional targeting problem]{ref}

<Gene> loss and <endosymbiotic gene transfer> are different: absence from an <organelle> <genome> does not by itself show that a functional nuclear copy exists. Conversely, an imported <protein> may derive from a host <gene> rather than a transferred <gene>. The surviving <organelle> <genome> is therefore a partial record, not a complete inventory of either ancestral <bacteria> or the modern <organelle>'s machinery. <Origin and Evolution of Plastids and Photosynthesis in Eukaryotes by Geoffrey I. McFadden>[“How to tame your symbiont”: losses, transfers, host recruitment and repatriation of proteins]{ref}

= Functional plastid-to-nucleus gene transfer experiment
{parent=Endosymbiotic gene transfer}

<Experimental Reconstruction of Functional Gene Transfer from the Tobacco Plastid Genome to the Nucleus by Sandra Stegemann and Ralph Bock> (2006, Results, pp. 2870–2874) separated relocation of <DNA> from activation of a relocated <gene>. Engineered tobacco <chloroplasts> carried an nptII <gene> with nuclear expression signals beside an aadA <gene> with plastid expression signals. Selection on kanamycin recovered plants with the segment in the nuclear <genome>. Crosses then replaced the engineered <chloroplasts> with ordinary <chloroplasts>: the nuclear segment remained, but the plants were initially sensitive to spectinomycin and streptomycin. The transferred aadA <gene> was not yet functional in its new setting.

Leaf pieces were cultured on regeneration medium containing both drugs. Resistant regenerated plants supplied the functional readout. Using both drugs reduced selection of spontaneous plastid <mutations> conferring resistance mainly to one drug. Reciprocal crosses and selfing distinguished maternal plastid inheritance from nuclear <Mendelian segregation>; eight of sixteen initial candidates had the latter pattern. <Experimental Reconstruction of Functional Gene Transfer from the Tobacco Plastid Genome to the Nucleus by Sandra Stegemann and Ralph Bock>[Figure 1, Tables 1–2 and Results, pp. 2870–2871: selection, eliminated candidates and inheritance controls]{ref}

The molecular readout came from <RNA> hybridization and <DNA sequencing>. A <Northern blot> detected aadA <messenger RNA> in activated lines, with different transcript sizes. Thermal asymmetric interlaced <polymerase chain reaction> recovered upstream <DNA>; products present in activated lines but absent in original-line and ordinary-plant controls were sequenced. The mapped rearrangements connected aadA to the upstream nuclear <gene promoter>. Thus resistance, inheritance, <RNA> detection and changed <nucleotide sequences> supported activation of the transferred nuclear <gene>, rather than resistance alone. <Experimental Reconstruction of Functional Gene Transfer from the Tobacco Plastid Genome to the Nucleus by Sandra Stegemann and Ralph Bock>[Figures 3–4, Results, pp. 2872–2874, and “PCR and DNA sequencing”/“TAIL-PCR”, pp. 2876–2877]{ref}

\Image[endosymbiosis-evidence-gene-transfer.png]
{title=Separate evidence for ancestry, <DNA> transfer and functional activation}
{description=Original evidence map and simplified experimental logic. Boxes summarize questions, readouts and controls; no measured <phylogenetic tree>, <nucleotide sequence>, deletion map or quantitative response is copied. <Organelle protein import> remains a separate question beyond this tobacco experiment.}

This engineered test demonstrates one route to functional nuclear <gene expression> after <plastid> <DNA> transfer. It does not establish a natural transfer rate, replay ancient <endosymbiosis> or demonstrate that the resulting <protein> is imported into a <chloroplast>. The authors explicitly identify acquisition of targeting information as an additional step not reproduced by this experiment. <Experimental Reconstruction of Functional Gene Transfer from the Tobacco Plastid Genome to the Nucleus by Sandra Stegemann and Ralph Bock>[Discussion, p. 2875: antibiotic selection and missing transit-peptide acquisition]{ref}

= Parasitism
{parent=Ecology}

<Parasitism> is an interaction in which a <parasite> obtains resources from a <host>, reducing some component of the <host>'s performance. Its demographic effects depend on exposure, within-host processes and transmission opportunities.

= Parasitoid
{parent=Parasitism}

A <parasitoid> develops on or in a <host> and eventually kills it. Host-parasitoid transmission therefore has different offspring accounting from ordinary <predation> or a nonlethal parasite-burden model.

= Host
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Parasitism}

= Host
{synonym}

A biological <host> provides the environment or resources used by a <parasite> or other associated organism.

= Parasite
{parent=Parasitism}

A <parasite> is an organism benefiting from <parasitism>. <Microparasite> and <macroparasite> distinguish useful transmission-model descriptions of within-host multiplication and distributed <parasite> burden.

= Macroparasite
{parent=Parasite}

A <macroparasite> is typically modelled using the number of <parasites> carried by each <host>. Adult parasitic worms illustrate why <within-host parasite burden>, developmental stages, mating and <host> exposure heterogeneity matter beyond an infected/not-infected classification.

= Within-host parasite burden
{parent=Macroparasite}
{title2=$j$}

The <within-host parasite burden> is the number of <parasites>, or a specified parasite-load measure, carried by one <host>. A population's <mean> burden and its infected-host <prevalence> are different summaries of the burden distribution.

= Microparasite
{parent=Parasite}

A <microparasite> is typically modelled as multiplying rapidly within a <host>, so infection status rather than the exact organism count is the first between-host state variable. <SIS model>, <SIR model> and <SEIR model> descriptions are examples. Additional within-host structure can still be required.

= Predation
{parent=Ecology}

<Predation> is the consumption of <prey> by a <predator>, affecting the demographic rates of both populations.

In the usual animal-feeding sense, a <predator> kills and eats its <prey>. This distinguishes the interaction from feeding on already-dead material and from the prolonged exploitation of a living <host> in <parasitism>. In the illustrative <food web>, a small <fish> eating <zooplankton> is a <predator>; a large <fish> eating that small <fish> makes the same small <fish> its <prey>. These roles belong to a particular interaction. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/45-6-community-ecology[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 45.6: predation and community interactions].

For a toy count, suppose 50 small <fish> begin a day and 8 are eaten by large <fish>. With births, other deaths and movement explicitly omitted, 42 small <fish> remain, and the fraction removed by <predation> is $8/50=0.16$. This measures removal of <prey>, not production of new <predators>. Food intake can support survival and reproduction, but eight meals do not imply eight new <predators>; <energy flow> and life-history details intervene. <Lotka-Volterra equations> provide one idealized account of such <population dynamics>, rather than a guarantee that every interacting pair must cycle.

= Prey
{parent=Predation}

<Prey> is an <organism> consumed by a <predator> in <predation>. The name describes the receiving end of that interaction: a small <fish> can be <prey> for a large <fish> while being a <predator> of <zooplankton>. In a <food web> drawn with resource-to-<consumer> arrows, a feeding arrow goes outward from the <prey> toward its <predator>. This convention makes the route of food and <energy> easy to follow.

= Predator
{parent=Predation}

A <predator> is an <organism> that consumes <prey> through <predation>. A <predator> need not occupy the highest <trophic level> of an <ecosystem>, and the label does not specify its size or guarantee that it has no <predators> of its own. In the illustrative <food chain>, the small <fish> is a <predator> at level 3 and also <prey> for the large <fish> at level 4. Separating these relational labels from <species> names prevents a misleading division of the world into permanent <predator> and <prey> categories.

\Include[protein]{parent=Biology}

= Morphogen
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

A <morphogen> is a signal whose spatial <concentration> helps specify different <biological cell> fates or behaviors during <biological development>. A <morphogen gradient> provides a positional cue: <biological cells> at different distances from a source experience different local <concentrations> and can follow different developmental pathways. A spatial <concentration> variation alone does not establish that a substance is a <morphogen>; its graded developmental effects matter. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26825/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition (2002): morphogens as graded developmental signals].

The beautiful idea is that one distributed signal can help organize several regions of a developing body. A continuous <morphogen gradient> can supply the input for distinct domains of <gene expression>, rather than requiring a separate signal for every position. A <morphogen response threshold> is a useful idealization of that interpretation: high, intermediate and low <concentrations> are assigned different outcomes. The <morphogen reaction-diffusion equation> describes one way to generate a <morphogen gradient>; the equation is a model of transport and reaction, while the <morphogen> is the signal being transported.

Detection and interpretation also depend on the receiving <biological cell>. For an extracellular <morphogen>, <receptors> and <signal transduction> connect the local <concentration> to a response; <receptor binding occupancy> is only one step in that connection. The same <morphogen gradient> can be interpreted differently in different tissues because their developmental context differs. Thus a <morphogen response threshold> summarizes a particular response in a particular context, rather than being a universal property of the signal. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9968/[Scott F. Gilbert, Developmental Biology, 6th edition (2000): positional information and context-dependent interpretation].

An important experimental example came in 1988. Wolfgang Driever and Christiane Nüsslein-Volhard used antibodies to examine the bicoid <protein> in early fruit-fly embryos. They reported a <concentration> profile approximately described by an <exponential function>, highest at the front, with the <protein> concentrated in <cell nuclei>. Their abstract proposed <diffusion> from localized <messenger RNA> and dispersed degradation as a likely origin of the <morphogen gradient>, rather than proving a unique transport mechanism. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3383244/[Driever and Nüsslein-Volhard, A gradient of bicoid protein in Drosophila embryos, Cell 54 (1988), 83–93: primary abstract]{ref}

In a companion study, Driever and Nüsslein-Volhard genetically changed the amount and distribution of bicoid <messenger RNA>, measured the resulting <protein> <morphogen gradient>, and compared developmental maps. Increased local bicoid <protein> levels shifted prospective anterior structures toward the rear; decreased levels shifted them toward the front. This linked a measured <concentration> profile to positional effects in <biological development>. The toy calculations below illustrate that logic with arbitrary numbers, not measured bicoid parameters. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3383245/[Driever and Nüsslein-Volhard, The bicoid protein determines position in the Drosophila embryo in a concentration-dependent manner, Cell 54 (1988), 95–104: primary abstract]{ref}

= Morphogen gradient
{parent=Morphogen}
{title2=$c(x)$}

A <morphogen gradient> is a spatially varying <concentration> profile of a <morphogen>. The profile $c(x)$ specifies the local amount; its mathematical <gradient>, $c'(x)$ in one dimension, specifies how rapidly that amount changes with position. In the simplest positional interpretation, a <biological cell> responds to its local <concentration>, without having to measure the <gradient> across itself. Production, transport and removal together determine a <morphogen gradient>.

For a worked example, take a stationary one-dimensional tissue occupying $x\geq0$, constant <diffusion coefficient> $D>0$, and uniform <first-order reaction> removal at rate $k>0$. At <steady state>, this specialization of the <morphogen reaction-diffusion equation> gives the <ordinary differential equation> and <boundary conditions>
$$
D\frac{d^2c}{dx^2}-kc=0,\qquad c(0)=c_0,\qquad \lim_{x\to\infty}c(x)=0.
$$
Here $D$ has units of length squared per time, $k$ has units of inverse time, and $c_0$ is a prescribed source <concentration>. The <Dirichlet boundary condition> fixes the <concentration> at $x=0$. It does not prescribe a source <flux>; any required <flux> follows from the solution. These assumptions define a toy <morphogen gradient>, not a universal account of transport during <biological development>.

Define the <diffusion length> $\ell=\sqrt{D/k}$. Substitution into the <ordinary differential equation> shows that its general solution is $A e^{x/\ell}+B e^{-x/\ell}$. The vanishing <boundary condition> at infinity forces $A=0$, and the source <Dirichlet boundary condition> forces $B=c_0$. Hence
$$
c(x)=c_0e^{-x/\ell},\qquad \ell=\sqrt{D/k}.
$$
Over every additional <diffusion length>, the <concentration> falls by the same factor $e^{-1}$. Faster <diffusion> increases $\ell$; faster removal decreases $\ell$. This makes the <diffusion length> a useful bridge between microscopic transport and the spatial reach of a <morphogen gradient>.

Choose $c_0=100$ arbitrary <concentration> units and $\ell=100\,\mu\mathrm m$, where $1\,\mu\mathrm m=10^{-6}\,\mathrm m$. Then $c(x)=100e^{-x/(100\,\mu\mathrm m)}$: at $x=0$, $100\,\mu\mathrm m$ and $200\,\mu\mathrm m$, the <concentrations> are $100$, approximately $36.79$ and approximately $13.53$. The following diagram combines this <morphogen gradient> with the idealized responses calculated under <morphogen response threshold>.

\Image[morphogen-gradient-and-thresholds.png]
{title=A toy <morphogen gradient> and three idealized developmental bands}
{description=The <steady state> profile uses $c_0=100$ arbitrary <concentration> units and <diffusion length> $100\,\mu\mathrm m$. <Morphogen response thresholds> at $50$ and $20$ place the boundaries at approximately $69.31$ and $160.94\,\mu\mathrm m$. The lower strip shows assigned fates A, B and C, not measured tissues. The plotted domain ends at $350\,\mu\mathrm m$; the mathematical domain continues to infinity.}

= Morphogen response threshold
{parent=Morphogen}
{title2=$\theta$}

A <morphogen response threshold> is a <concentration> level used to distinguish developmental responses to a <morphogen>. In an idealized threshold model, otherwise equivalent <biological cells> exposed to $c\geq\theta$ take one response and those exposed to $c<\theta$ take another. Two <morphogen response thresholds> can assign three bands: fate A for $c\geq50$, fate B for $20\leq c<50$, and fate C for $c<20$, all in the toy arbitrary <concentration> units. The A/B/C labels specify a response rule, not named cell types or a claim that every <morphogen> produces sharp boundaries. See the positional-information model in Gilbert's reference under <morphogen>.

For the decreasing <morphogen gradient> $c(x)=c_0e^{-x/\ell}$ and $0<\theta<c_0$, solve $c(x_\theta)=\theta$ using the <natural logarithm>:
$$
x_\theta=\ell\ln\!\left(\frac{c_0}{\theta}\right).
$$
With $c_0=100$ and <diffusion length> $\ell=100\,\mu\mathrm m$, the <morphogen response thresholds> $50$ and $20$ give
$$
x_{50}=100\ln2\,\mu\mathrm m\approx69.3147\,\mu\mathrm m,\qquad x_{20}=100\ln5\,\mu\mathrm m\approx160.9438\,\mu\mathrm m.
$$
Thus fate A occupies $0\leq x\leq x_{50}$, fate B occupies $x_{50}<x\leq x_{20}$, and fate C occupies $x>x_{20}$ in this idealization. The equality convention belongs to the response rule. If $\theta=c_0$, the crossing is at the source; if $\theta>c_0$, that <morphogen response threshold> is never reached anywhere in the tissue.

Now double the prescribed source <concentration> from $c_0$ to $2c_0$, keeping $D$, $k$, <diffusion length> $\ell$ and a fixed <morphogen response threshold> $0<\theta<c_0$ unchanged. The whole <morphogen gradient> doubles, but the boundary moves by an additive distance:
$$
\Delta x_\theta=\ell\ln\!\left(\frac{2c_0}{\theta}\right)-\ell\ln\!\left(\frac{c_0}{\theta}\right)=\ell\ln2.
$$
For $\theta=20$, the crossing moves from $160.9438\,\mu\mathrm m$ to $100\ln10\,\mu\mathrm m\approx230.2585\,\mu\mathrm m$, a shift of $69.3147\,\mu\mathrm m$. Every fixed <morphogen response threshold> below the original source <concentration> shifts by this same distance in the toy model. The example compares two <Dirichlet boundary conditions>, $c(0)=100$ and $c(0)=200$; doubling an experimentally supplied source <flux> is a different boundary specification.

\Image[morphogen-source-strength-boundary-shift.png]
{title=Doubling source <concentration> shifts a toy <morphogen response threshold> boundary}
{description=Profiles with source <concentrations> $100$ and $200$ share <diffusion length> $100\,\mu\mathrm m$. The same <morphogen response threshold> $20$ is crossed at approximately $160.94$ and $230.26\,\mu\mathrm m$. The arrow marks the predicted $69.31\,\mu\mathrm m$ shift. Source strength here means prescribed boundary <concentration>, with all numbers arbitrary and illustrative.}

These calculations are useful because they separate changes in source <concentration>, <diffusion length> and response rule. Their limits are equally concrete: growing tissues change the geometry, a <transient response> can precede <steady state>, molecular fluctuations can blur a boundary, and <signaling feedback> or altered transport can change the <morphogen gradient> or its interpretation. A local instantaneous <morphogen response threshold> need not capture exposure duration, <signaling adaptation> or the receiving <biological cell>'s state. Experiments on cultured human <biological cells> found different responses to the level and rate of change of developmental signals, illustrating why signal dynamics can matter. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6398983/[Heemskerk et al., Rapid changes in morphogen concentration control self-organized patterning in human embryonic stem cells, eLife 8 (2019), e40526: primary experiments]{ref} Quantitative measurements of bicoid <protein> and its downstream <gene expression> also make precision a question for experiment, rather than something guaranteed by drawing threshold lines. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2253670/[Gregor et al., Probing the limits to positional information, Cell 130 (2007), 153–164: primary measurements]{ref} <Morphogen gradients> provide one mechanism for spatial patterning in <biological development>, alongside other signals and interactions.

= Twin
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

Twins are two offspring from the same gestation. <Monozygotic twins> arise from one fertilized egg, while <dizygotic twins> arise from two. The distinction supplies the biological premise for <twin studies>.

= Biosignature
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

A <biosignature> is observable chemical, morphological or other evidence attributable to biological activity after assessing plausible abiotic explanations. It can concern present or past life on Earth or elsewhere. An <exoplanet biosignature> is the remote planetary specialization, requiring atmospheric and environmental context.

= Mortality
{parent=Biology}

Mortality describes deaths in a defined population during a specified observation window. A cumulative death <probability> is distinct from a death rate per person-time. Comparing treatments requires the same endpoint definition, time origin and ascertainment in both arms.

\Include[neuroscience]{parent=Biology}

= Enzyme
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

An <enzyme> is a biological <catalyst>: it accelerates a <chemical reaction> and is regenerated after a successful catalytic cycle. Most <enzymes> are <proteins>, but catalytic <RNA> molecules, called <ribozymes>, also exist. Many reactions needed by a <biological cell> would proceed too slowly under its ordinary conditions without <enzymes>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/[Cooper, The Cell: the central role of enzymes as biological catalysts].

The biological beauty is selective speed. A <biological cell> contains many possible <reactants>; its <enzymes> help determine which transformations happen on a useful timescale. In <metabolism>, one step's <reaction product> can become another step's <substrate>, so selective catalysts support an organized flow of material.

The <ribosome> is a striking biological example: the machinery that builds <proteins> has <RNA> at its catalytic centre. The complete <ribosome> contains both <RNA> and <proteins>, but its bond-forming <active site> is built from <RNA>. Structural evidence established this distinction in https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10937990/[Nissen et al., The structural basis of ribosome activity in peptide bond synthesis, Science 289, 920–930 (2000)].

Selection occurs at an <active site>, the region where <substrate> binding and catalytic chemistry take place. Shape and chemical interactions favour some <substrates> over others; binding can also reshape the <enzyme>, an <induced fit>. Recognition alone is insufficient: holding a <substrate> extremely tightly need not make it react quickly. Catalysis requires a favourable route through the <transition state>, the high-barrier configuration between <reactants> and <reaction products>. Positioning reactive groups and stabilizing this configuration relative to the bound <substrate> can lower that barrier. Specificity is often strong, but need not mean exactly one acceptable <substrate>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/[Cooper, The Cell: mechanisms of enzymatic catalysis].

\Image[enzyme-catalysis-energy.png]
{title=<Enzymes> lower reaction barriers while leaving the overall <Gibbs free energy> difference unchanged}
{description=Original illustrative profiles, with the free <enzyme> included in both endpoint states. The horizontal coordinate orders configurations along each route; it is neither elapsed time nor a measured distance. Actual catalytic mechanisms can have several intermediates and barriers.}

Read the diagram numerically. Its chosen <Gibbs free energies> are $0$ for the <reactants> and $-10\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ for the <reaction products>. The uncatalysed peak is $60\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, whereas the catalysed peak is $40\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The forward free-energy barriers are therefore $60$ and $40\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$; viewed from the <reaction products>, the reverse barriers are $70$ and $50\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Both decrease by $20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, while the endpoint difference stays $-10\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. These are constructed values, not measurements of a particular <enzyme>.

This separates <chemical kinetics> from <chemical equilibrium>. An <enzyme> changes how rapidly the system approaches <chemical equilibrium>; it does not change the <equilibrium constant> for the same overall <chemical reaction> under the same conditions. Faster catalysis therefore supplies no extra driving <energy>. In a <biological cell>, continuing supply of <reactants> and removal of <reaction products> can nevertheless sustain a net flow: the surroundings keep that system away from <chemical equilibrium>. See https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/kinetics/ek1t3.html[IUBMB, Symbolism and terminology in enzyme kinetics: basic definitions].

Some <enzymes> require a <cofactor (biochemistry)>, an additional nonprotein component such as a metal <ion> or a small organic molecule. Organic helpers are often called <coenzymes>; they can assist transfers that the <protein> alone cannot readily perform. The <cofactor (biochemistry)> may remain bound or exchange with the surroundings, depending on the system. A <coenzyme> can be chemically changed in one reaction and regenerated by another: regeneration of the <enzyme> does not mean every helper is unchanged in that individual reaction. Some <enzymes> need no additional <cofactor (biochemistry)>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26911/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: small molecules associated with proteins] and https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/[Alberts et al., activated carriers and their regeneration].

How much faster does adding <substrate> make a reaction? <Enzyme kinetics> gives a quantitative answer when a model's assumptions fit. For an illustrative reaction obeying <Michaelis-Menten kinetics>, use the <Michaelis-Menten equation> with <substrate> <concentration> $s$, limiting <reaction rate> $V_{\max}$ and <Michaelis constant> $K_M$. At $s/K_M=0.1$, substitution gives $v/V_{\max}=0.1/1.1=1/11\approx0.0909$. At $s/K_M=1$, it gives $1/2$; at $s/K_M=10$, it gives $10/11\approx0.9091$. Thus a hundredfold increase in <substrate> between the first and last examples yields only a tenfold increase in <reaction rate>.

\Image[enzyme-substrate-saturation.png]
{title=Illustrative <Michaelis-Menten kinetics>: $s/K_M=0.1,1,10$ gives $v/V_{\max}=1/11,1/2,10/11$}
{description=Original normalized curve and computed examples, with a logarithmic concentration axis to separate the three values. The dashed upper line is a limiting rate approached at high <substrate> <concentration>, not reached at a finite concentration in this model.}

For a chosen $V_{\max}=110\,\mathrm{\mu M\,min^{-1}}$, those fractions mean <reaction rates> of $10$, $55$ and $100\,\mathrm{\mu M\,min^{-1}}$. Doubling <substrate> from $10K_M$ to $20K_M$ raises the normalized rate only from $10/11$ to $20/21\approx0.9524$, a relative increase of about $4.76\%$. In this simplest model, the catalytic machinery is already nearly fully occupied. Doubling the amount of active <enzyme> instead doubles $V_{\max}$ and the predicted <reaction rates> at fixed free <substrate> <concentration>, provided the other conditions are unchanged. This distinction helps decide whether additional <substrate> or additional catalytic capacity could improve throughput.

The interpretation has limits. The simplest <Michaelis-Menten kinetics> assumes a single <substrate>, fixed active <enzyme> amount, a suitable <quasi-steady-state approximation>, and initial measurements with negligible reverse reaction from accumulated <reaction product>. Substantial depletion, interacting binding sites, or inhibition by excess <substrate> can invalidate this model. Within it, $K_M$ is the <substrate> <concentration> giving half the limiting <reaction rate>; it is not generally the binding <dissociation constant> $K_d$. See https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/kinetics/ek1t3.html[IUBMB, enzyme kinetics: steady-state approximation and substrate dissociation] and https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/kinetics/ek4t6.html[IUBMB, Michaelis-Menten and non-Michaelis-Menten kinetics]. Measuring activity also requires specified <temperature>, <pH> and necessary <cofactor (biochemistry)> availability: a weak signal can reflect unsuitable conditions rather than little <enzyme>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK92007/[Brooks et al., Assay Guidance Manual: basics of enzymatic assays for HTS].

= Protein kinase
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

A <protein kinase> is an <enzyme> that catalyzes <phosphorylation> of a <protein>. In <receptor tyrosine kinase> signaling, <ATP> supplies the transferred <phosphate> group.

= Autophosphorylation
{parent=Protein kinase}
{wiki}

<Autophosphorylation> is <phosphorylation> of a <protein kinase> by its own catalytic activity or that of another member of the same <protein kinase> assembly. Distinguish modification within one chain from <trans-autophosphorylation> between partners.

= Trans-autophosphorylation
{parent=Autophosphorylation}

In <trans-autophosphorylation>, one <protein kinase> chain phosphorylates another partner chain. “Auto” therefore does not require the modified site and catalytic <active site> to be on the same chain. The <insulin receptor> provides an activation-loop example; <EGFR> shows why <autophosphorylation> and catalytic activation need not be the same event.

= Protein phosphatase
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

A <protein phosphatase> is an <enzyme> that removes a <phosphate> group from a modified <protein> through <hydrolysis>. This restores a modifiable site rather than regenerating <ATP> by simply running <protein kinase> chemistry backward.

= Protein tyrosine phosphatase
{parent=Protein phosphatase}
{wiki}

A <protein tyrosine phosphatase> acts on <phosphotyrosine residues>. Removal can undo a particular receptor modification; its consequences for the whole <signal transduction> pathway depend on the target.

= Phosphoinositide 3-kinase
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

= Protein kinase B
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

= Akt
{c}
{synonym}

= Adenylyl cyclase
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

= Protein kinase A
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

= Phosphorylase kinase
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

= Glycogen phosphorylase
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

<Glycogen phosphorylase> is an <enzyme> involved in <glycogen> breakdown. Its activity is the readout in the extract-transfer experiment under <second messenger>; see that experiment's primary references. A change in <enzyme> activity need not mean production of more <enzyme> <protein>.

= Ribozyme
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

A <ribozyme> is an <RNA> <molecule> whose folded structure provides <catalysis> of a <chemical reaction>. A <protein> <enzyme> builds its <active site> from an <amino acid> chain; a <ribozyme> builds a working <active site> from <RNA>, sometimes with metal <ions> and associated <proteins>. <Base pairing> can help recognize a <substrate>, but recognition alone does not establish <catalysis>: the <RNA> must promote chemical conversion. A passive <messenger RNA> template being read by a <ribosome> is not thereby a <ribozyme>. See https://herschlaglab.stanford.edu/s/39.pdf[Cech and Herschlag, Group I Ribozymes: Substrate Recognition, Catalytic Strategies, and Comparative Mechanistic Analysis, sections 1–3].

The surprise is that an information-bearing <polymer> can also perform chemistry. Examples include the self-splicing <group I intron> of Tetrahymena thermophila and the catalytic <RNA> component of bacterial <RNase P>, discussed below. The <ribosome> supplies an everyday example: its <peptide bond>-forming <active site> is made of <ribosomal RNA>, although the complete machine also contains <proteins>. Nissen and colleagues examined structures of a large <ribosome> subunit with <substrate> analogues and found <RNA> contacting those analogues at the catalytic centre. This supports an <RNA> <active site>, not a claim that isolated <ribosomal RNA> can carry out all of <biological translation>. See https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10937990/[Nissen, Hansen, Ban, Moore and Steitz, The structural basis of ribosome activity in peptide bond synthesis, Science 289 (2000), 920–930].

For the Tetrahymena <group I intron>, <RNA splicing> removes the <intron> and joins its flanking <exons>. The first chemical step uses the free $3'$ <hydroxyl group> of an external <guanosine> to attack the <phosphodiester bond> at the $5'$ splice junction. This releases the upstream <exon> with a free $3'$ <hydroxyl group> and attaches the <guanosine> to the <intron>. That newly freed <hydroxyl group> then attacks the <phosphodiester bond> at the other splice junction, joining the <exons> and releasing the linear <intron>. Each step exchanges one backbone connection for another; it is not simply separation of <base pairs>. See https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6101203/[Cech, Zaug and Grabowski, 1981: the phosphoester-transfer mechanism].

\Image[ribozymes-splice-bond-map.png]
{title=<Group I intron> self-splicing: an external <guanosine> starts two exchanges of <phosphodiester bonds>}
{description=Original chemical-connectivity schematic, not an atomic structure or measured reaction trace. Orange junctions are the two bonds replaced; green junctions are the new bonds. The folded <RNA> <active site> and <magnesium> <ions> are omitted. The linear excised <intron> is shown before any subsequent circularization.}

The motivating experiment began with an unexpected control. As Cech recounts, Zaug purified a <ribosomal RNA> precursor from Tetrahymena using a detergent and phenol extraction, then compared test tubes with and without added <cell nucleus> extract. Incubation in salts and <nucleotides> released the same amount of small <intron> <RNA> in both cases, detected by <gel electrophoresis>. Repetition made a missing ingredient or accidental omission less plausible. Activity also survived treatments intended to destroy <proteins>. Nevertheless, absence of added extract did not by itself exclude a tightly attached, resistant <protein>, or prove that the small <RNA> had been newly excised rather than released from an already processed complex. See https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/cech-lecture.pdf[Thomas R. Cech, 1989 Nobel lecture, pp. 655–659: the unexpected control and alternative explanations].

An independent chemical readout addressed the latter problem. Cech mixed precursor <RNA> with <phosphorus>-32-labelled <guanosine> bearing three <phosphate> groups (GTP), and used <gel electrophoresis> followed by exposure to X-ray film to locate the label. Label appeared in the excised <intron> with <magnesium> present; it was absent when <magnesium> was omitted or labelled <ATP> replaced the labelled <guanosine> reagent. This experiment is described in https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/cech-lecture.pdf[Cech, 1989 Nobel lecture, pp. 657–658: labelled-guanosine controls]. The 1981 study established attachment through the <guanosine> $3'$ <hydroxyl group> and showed that <guanosine> without a triphosphate also supported excision: breaking down the added <nucleotide> to supply <energy> was unnecessary. See https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6101203/[Cech, Zaug and Grabowski, In vitro splicing of the ribosomal RNA precursor of Tetrahymena, Cell 27 (1981), 487–496: primary abstract]. Separately, sequencing and terminal analysis found an extra $5'$ <guanosine> absent from the corresponding <DNA> sequence. See https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7099968/[Zaug and Cech, The intervening sequence excised from the ribosomal RNA precursor of Tetrahymena contains a 5-terminal guanosine residue not encoded by the DNA, Nucleic Acids Research 10 (1982), 2823–2838]. Together, incorporation and sequence evidence support a new <covalent bond>, beyond a change in how <RNA> associates.

Kruger, Grabowski, Zaug, Sands, Gottschling and Cech's 1982 test removed dependence on a precursor purified from Tetrahymena. They cloned the <intron> and adjacent <exons> into a <DNA> template, made precursor <RNA> with purified bacterial <RNA polymerase> under conditions suppressing <RNA splicing>, and removed <proteins> by detergent–phenol extraction. Denaturing <gel electrophoresis> separated precursor species. Purified precursor was then incubated with the <guanosine> reagent, <magnesium> and salt; matched samples without this incubation supplied a precursor baseline. Their standard assay used $10\,\mathrm{mM}$ magnesium chloride, $0.1\,\mathrm{mM}$ of that <guanosine> reagent, $100\,\mathrm{mM}$ ammonium sulfate and a <buffer solution> at <pH> $7.5$, for $30$ minutes at <temperature> $37\,{}^{\circ}\mathrm C$, with specified variations in individual experiments. <Gel electrophoresis> and radioactive-film detection showed excised <intron> <RNA>; sequencing verified its $5'$ junction. See https://dosequis.colorado.edu/courses/methodslogic/papers/Cech1982.pdf[Kruger et al., Self-Splicing RNA, Cell 31 (1982), 147–157, figures 3–4 and Experimental Procedures].

Excision alone could leave disconnected <exons>. To test joining, the authors paired labelled <RNA> with <DNA> spanning the joined-<exon> sequence, removed exposed <RNA> with <enzymes>, and separated the protected pieces by denaturing <gel electrophoresis>. Spliced samples yielded protected <RNA> long enough to span the junction. See https://dosequis.colorado.edu/courses/methodslogic/papers/Cech1982.pdf[Kruger et al., 1982, figure 6: independent evidence for exon ligation]. The experimental logic matters: an <intron>-sized band identifies a candidate product, while sequence and junction assays test its chemical identity.

These controls support sufficiency of the prepared <RNA> under the assay conditions. They do not show that every <RNA> self-splices, or that <proteins> are dispensable inside a <biological cell>. Many <group I introns> use <proteins> to help attain an active fold. See https://herschlaglab.stanford.edu/s/39.pdf[Cech and Herschlag, Group I Ribozymes, section 6: protein facilitation]. Nor does a failed assay necessarily identify an inactive <nucleotide sequence>: unsuitable <ions>, <pH>, or <temperature> can prevent a working <active site> from forming.

The distinction between self-processing and repeated use became especially clear with bacterial <RNase P>. In 1983 Guerrier-Takada, Gardiner, Marsh, Pace and Altman separated the <RNA> and <protein> components of <RNase P> from two <bacteria>. Purified <RNA> cleaved precursor <transfer RNA> under conditions containing $60\,\mathrm{mM}$ <magnesium> <ions>, or $10\,\mathrm{mM}$ <magnesium> <ions> plus $1\,\mathrm{mM}$ spermidine. The <protein> components alone had no catalytic activity. At $5$–$10\,\mathrm{mM}$ <magnesium> without spermidine, both components were needed. Thus the comparison demonstrated an <RNA> <catalyst> while also explaining why <protein> assistance could matter under other conditions. <RNase P> produces the mature $5'$ end of <transfer RNA> by removing its leader; it acts on a separate <substrate> and can be used again. See https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6197186/[Guerrier-Takada et al., The RNA moiety of ribonuclease P is the catalytic subunit of the enzyme, Cell 35 (1983), 849–857: primary abstract].

Self-splicing demonstrates <RNA> chemistry, but one precursor completing one rearrangement is not yet evidence for catalytic turnover: repeated conversion of separate <substrates> by a regenerated <catalyst>. The self-splicing precursor changes its own connectivity and incorporates external <guanosine>. In 1986 Zaug and Cech showed that a shortened Tetrahymena <intron> <RNA> could cleave and rejoin separate short <RNA> <substrates> in a sequence-dependent reaction. This demonstrated an <RNA> <enzyme> acting beyond its own self-processing. See https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3941911/[Zaug and Cech, The intervening sequence RNA of Tetrahymena is an enzyme, Science 231 (1986), 470–475: primary abstract].

\Image[ribozymes-turnover-model.png]
{title=Stipulated <chemical kinetics> models: one self-processing event versus repeated <ribozyme> turnover}
{description=Original analytical illustration, with dimensionless time and amounts normalized by the initial <RNA> amount. Curves are calculated from the equations below, not experimental data or fits to Tetrahymena or <RNase P>. Equal exponential timescales are a chosen teaching convention; the figure does not compare their biological speeds.}

Read the second figure as a counting argument. In a stipulated model, $A$ denotes unprocessed precursor and $P_{\rm self}$ counts completed self-processing events. Set $A(0)=R_0$, $P_{\rm self}(0)=0$ and $dA/d\tau=-A$. The <exponential function> solution gives $P_{\rm self}/R_0=1-e^{-\tau}$, never exceeding one. Here <guanosine> and other necessary reagents are taken to be available in excess; $P_{\rm self}$ counts events, not every separate <reaction product> <molecule>.

For a second stipulated model, let an unchanged <ribozyme> $R$ perform $R+S\longrightarrow R+P$ on a separate <substrate> $S$. Assume the effective <law of mass action> $dS/dt=-kRS$, no reverse reaction, no <ribozyme> loss and no inhibited or long-lived bound states. Set $R=R_0$, $S(0)=5R_0$, $P(0)=0$ and $\tau=kR_0t$. Then $S/R_0=5e^{-\tau}$ and $P/R_0=5(1-e^{-\tau})$, with $S+P=5R_0$. At $\tau=1$, self-processing gives about $0.632$ completed events per initial <RNA>, whereas the regenerated <ribozyme> gives about $3.161$ <reaction product> <molecules> per initial <ribozyme>. More than one <substrate> conversion per initial <ribozyme>, with appropriate background and identity controls, is evidence for turnover. Product accumulation below that threshold alone cannot distinguish a reusable <catalyst> from one-shot chemistry. These models illustrate that distinction; actual <enzyme kinetics> can include folding delays, binding, saturation and product release.

= Ribonuclease P
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

= RNase P
{c}
{synonym}

An <enzyme> that processes the $5'$ end of precursor <transfer RNA>. The bacterial <RNase P> used in the discovery experiments contains catalytic <RNA> and a supporting <protein>; the <ribozyme> body gives the conditions under which its <RNA> component works alone. This description concerns those bacterial complexes, not every form of <RNase P>.

= Active site
{parent=Enzyme}
{wiki}

An <active site> is the region of an <enzyme> where a <substrate> binds and undergoes a <chemical reaction>. In a <protein> <enzyme>, it includes the <amino acid residues> that contact the <substrate> and those that directly assist <catalysis>. These roles can overlap: an <amino acid side chain> can both orient a <substrate> and participate in chemistry. The <active site> occupies only part of the folded <protein>; the remaining <amino acid residues> help establish its <protein structure>, stability and movement. An <active site> need not be a consecutive stretch of <protein primary structure>: <protein folding> can bring distant <amino acid residues> together. A <ribozyme> instead supplies an <RNA> <active site>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26911/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell, Protein Function: binding sites and catalytic sites].

Binding and <catalysis> answer different questions. A <protein binding site> recognizes shape and chemistry through such interactions as <hydrogen bonds>, <electrostatics> and the <hydrophobic effect>. At an <active site>, appropriate positioning also helps a <substrate> reach the <transition state> of a <chemical reaction>. Some <amino acid side chains> transfer a <proton>; others participate transiently in a <covalent bond>. The <enzyme-substrate complex> is the associated <enzyme> and <substrate>, before conversion to a <reaction product>. Merely accumulating this complex does not establish rapid <catalysis>: stabilizing the bound <substrate> without sufficiently stabilizing the <transition state> can leave a large barrier. The important complementarity is chemical as well as geometric. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9921/[Geoffrey M. Cooper, The Cell, The Central Role of Enzymes as Biological Catalysts: substrate binding and transition-state stabilization].

\Image[active-sites-induced-fit-residue-map.png]
{title=Distant <amino acid residues> can cooperate at an <active site>}
{description=Original twelve-residue <polypeptide chain> cartoon, with the same order and eleven connections in both drawings. Positions 3, 7 and 10 approach a generic <substrate> after an imagined fold. Blue means recognition, orange means catalytic chemistry, and purple means both; these assignments are invented for explanation. Dashed contacts represent cooperation, not specified <hydrogen bonds> or actual atomic coordinates. The other nine positions support the imagined <protein structure>; three highlighted positions are not a universal <active site> size.}

The cartoon makes a useful experimental distinction. Changing a contacting <amino acid residue> might weaken <substrate> binding, change the <reaction rate>, or do both. Changing a distant <amino acid residue> can also disrupt the <active site> by changing <protein folding>. Therefore a loss of activity after a <mutation> alone does not locate a catalytic group: compare binding, <protein structure> and <enzyme kinetics> as well. This is an interpretation guide, not a report of a particular <mutation> experiment.

A concrete example is <hexokinase>: it transfers the terminal <phosphate> group of <ATP> to the $6$-position <hydroxyl group> of a <sugar> such as <glucose>. Specificity need not mean only one acceptable <substrate>: the <hexokinase> reaction class includes several possible acceptor <sugars>. Which are used, and at what <reaction rates>, depends on the particular <enzyme> and conditions. See https://enzyme.expasy.org/EC/2.7.1.1[Swiss Institute of Bioinformatics, ENZYME entry EC 2.7.1.1: hexokinase reaction and acceptor range].

Bennett and Steitz compared <yeast> <hexokinase> A crystallized with <glucose> against <hexokinase> B crystallized without <glucose>. <X-rays> scattered by the crystals supplied <diffraction> measurements from which the authors reconstructed <protein structure>; comparing coordinates located movement, rather than measuring a <reaction rate>. The <glucose>-containing cleft was narrower: one lobe was rotated by about $12^\circ$ relative to the other, with some <protein backbone> displacements reaching $8\,\mathrm{\mathring A}$. This makes the <active site> a moving arrangement of interacting groups, rather than a permanent hole. See https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC336218/[William S. Bennett Jr. and Thomas A. Steitz, Glucose-induced conformational change in yeast hexokinase, PNAS 75 (1978), 4848–4852].

The structural comparison has two explicit limitations: different forms of <hexokinase> and different crystal environments were compared, and static coordinates do not record the order of binding and movement. Closure is consistent with <induced fit>, but those structures alone cannot exclude <conformational selection>. The solution experiment under <induced fit> addresses whether compaction also occurs outside a crystal.

= Induced fit
{parent=Active site}

In <induced fit>, a <substrate> binds to an <enzyme> and the resulting <enzyme-substrate complex> subsequently changes its <protein structure>. This adjustment can position interacting groups at the <active site> for <catalysis>. The mechanistic claim concerns order: binding precedes the relevant change. In <conformational selection>, the unbound <enzyme> already visits an alternative <protein structure>, which the <substrate> preferentially binds. Both processes can occur in one system. Let $E$ and $E^*$ denote two structures of the same <enzyme>, and $S$ a <substrate>. The limiting routes are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{induced fit:}\quad &E+S\rightleftharpoons ES\rightleftharpoons E^*S,\\
\text{conformational selection:}\quad &E\rightleftharpoons E^*,\qquad E^*+S\rightleftharpoons E^*S.
\end{aligned}
$$
The star labels a structure, not a different <protein primary structure>, and does not by itself mean catalytic activity. Each reversible arrow abbreviates a step, not the whole catalytic cycle. See https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2728963/[Gordon G. Hammes, Yu-Chu Chang and Terrence G. Oas, Conformational selection or induced fit: a flux description of reaction mechanism, PNAS 106 (2009), 13737–13741].

A rigid lock-and-key cartoon usefully suggests selective <substrate> recognition, but it suppresses movement of the <protein> and chemical changes at the <active site>. Replacing it with two static drawings, “open” and “closed”, still does not establish <induced fit>: both routes above can end at $E^*S$. Also, if complete closure prevents a <substrate> entering, the pre-existing structure selected in <conformational selection> might be partly open and undergo further <induced fit> after binding. The usefulness of the distinction is that it turns an attractive shape story into questions about <enzyme kinetics>. Relative traffic through the routes depends on populations, <rate constants> and free <substrate> <concentration>, rather than affinity alone. See https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2728963/[Hammes, Chang and Oas, 2009: coupled pathways, their concentrations and the limitations of endpoint structures].

\Image[active-sites-induced-fit-binding-orders.png]
{title=<Induced fit> and <conformational selection>: two orders, one bound endpoint}
{description=Original reaction-order schematic and an analytical first-event model, not a <hexokinase> structure or measured data. In the upper square, horizontal steps bind or release a <substrate>; vertical steps change <protein structure>. In the lower plot, an initially unbound $E$ can bind with rate $a=k_{\mathrm{on}}s$ or change structure with rate $b$, for chosen $k_{\mathrm{on}}=1\,\mathrm{\mu M^{-1}s^{-1}}$ and $b=1\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. The plotted <probability> $a/(a+b)$ concerns only the first event from $E$, not the fraction completing either full route. Subsequent binding, reversals and <catalysis> are not modelled by that curve.}

For this analytical illustration, hold the free <substrate> <concentration> $s$ constant and assume independent waiting times with <exponential distributions> of constant rates. The <law of mass action> gives the binding rate $a=k_{\mathrm{on}}s$; the competing change has rate $b$. The <probability density function> for binding to happen first at time $t$ is $a e^{-(a+b)t}$, because neither event has happened earlier. Its integral is
$$
P(\text{binding first})=\int_0^\infty a e^{-(a+b)t}\,dt=\frac{a}{a+b}.
$$
With the stated invented <rate constants>, $s=0.1,1,10\,\mathrm{\mu M}$ gives $1/11,1/2,10/11$, respectively. Raising free <substrate> <concentration> makes binding more likely to win this initial competition. This <probability> is deliberately narrower than a full-route fraction: returns to $E$, later changes and the binding properties of $E^*$ would all matter for the latter. The illustration explains why timing and <concentration> must accompany structural evidence, without assigning a binding mechanism to a real <enzyme>.

A motivating experiment tested the crystal interpretation in solution. McDonald, Steitz and Engelman prepared solutions of monomeric <yeast> <hexokinase> B and compared the unbound preparation with sugar-bound preparations. Their apparatus measured small-angle scattering of <X-rays>; the spatial extent inferred from that readout was the <radius of gyration>, rather than a catalytic <reaction rate>. Binding <glucose> reduced the <radius of gyration> by $0.95\pm0.24\,\mathrm{\mathring A}$; binding the phosphorylated <glucose> product reduced it by $1.25\pm0.28\,\mathrm{\mathring A}$, as reported by the authors. The <glucose> change agreed with the reduction calculated from the crystal coordinates. See https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/369601/[Robert C. McDonald, Thomas A. Steitz and Donald M. Engelman, Yeast hexokinase in solution exhibits a large conformational change upon binding glucose or glucose 6-phosphate, Biochemistry 18 (1979), 338–342: primary abstract].

The comparison without added <substrate> supplies a baseline within the same form of <hexokinase>; the second bound preparation tests another binding condition. Together with the crystal comparison, solution compaction supports closure beyond crystal packing. It still gives an average spatial readout, not a movie of individual binding events, and product-associated compaction does not demonstrate ongoing <catalysis>. Neither experiment alone proves that binding precedes movement. To distinguish <induced fit> from <conformational selection>, structural comparisons must be combined with measurements sensitive to timing and with a model of the relevant populations and <enzyme kinetics>.

= Enzyme-substrate complex
{parent=Active site}
{title2=$ES$}

